Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — this is a good time to get organized. Before I create a detailed checklist and suggested questions, one quick clarification: you mentioned family court in Denver and also Hennepin County, Minnesota. Which court is actually handling this case (Denver/Colorado or Hennepin County/Minnesota)? The specific jurisdiction changes some deadlines, forms, and local practices.

Below is a practical, ready-to-use plan you can bring to your lawyer (Robert Kim) and to the status conference. If you confirm the correct jurisdiction I’ll tailor the items further.

What to ask your attorney (high-priority questions)
- Jurisdiction & venue
  - Which court has jurisdiction here (Denver or Hennepin)? Are there any jurisdictional issues to worry about?
- Case status & strategy
  - What is our immediate goal for the status conference in two months?
  - What is our overall strategy (settlement, mediation, trial)?
  - What are the realistic outcomes for custody, parenting time, and support?
- Timeline & deadlines
  - What are the next procedural steps and timelines (temporary orders, discovery, mediation, trial estimate)?
  - Are there upcoming filing deadlines I need to meet before the status conference?
- Temporary/ emergency relief
  - Should we ask for temporary custody, parenting time, child support, or exclusive use of the home?
  - If there’s urgent safety/relocation risk, what emergency options exist?
- Custody & parenting time specifics
  - How does Minnesota/Colorado law (confirm jurisdiction) approach custody factors?
  - What is the plan for a parenting plan and what factors do judges prioritize?
  - Could a custody evaluation or Guardian ad Litem be ordered — when and how?
- Child support & financials
  - How is child support calculated in this jurisdiction and what documents do you need?
  - Should we request temporary support now? What about spousal maintenance?
- Discovery & evidence
  - What discovery should we start now (financial disclosures, text messages, school records, medical records)?
  - What evidence will be most persuasive (communications, calendars, witness statements)?
- Settlement & alternative dispute resolution (ADR)
  - Is mediation or court-ordered settlement conference likely/required?
  - What are the costs and likely timeline of mediation vs going to trial?
- Costs & billing
  - What will typical costs look like from now until the status conference and beyond?
  - Are there ways to reduce cost (narrow issues, mediation)?
- Next steps you should take
  - What should I do between now and the status conference to strengthen my case?

Documents and information to gather now
- Identification & case basics
  - Court paperwork you’ve already received/served (summons, petitions, temporary orders)
  - Names, ages, birth certificates of children
- Financial documents (last 2–3 years if possible)
  - Recent pay stubs, W-2s, 1099s, tax returns
  - Bank & investment statements, retirement, mortgage/lease statements
  - Childcare costs, medical premiums/out-of-pocket costs, school or extracurricular expenses
  - Any spousal support history or prior agreements
- Parenting & custody evidence
  - A calendar of parenting time and important events showing who did what and when
  - Texts, emails, social media posts that are relevant (keep originals)
  - School, medical, daycare records showing attendance, involvement and needs
  - Photos, GPS or travel proof only if directly relevant and lawful to use
- Safety concerns
  - Any police reports, restraining orders, or healthcare documentation if safety/abuse is an issue
- Witnesses
  - Names and contact info for people who can testify about parenting, home environment, or finances (teachers, coaches, neighbors, family)

What to prepare for the status conference
- Short written summary for the judge (often 1–2 pages): current situation, issues in dispute, what relief you’re asking for, what you want to accomplish next
- Proposed timeline/order that you want the court to set (deadlines for discovery, mediation, custody evaluations)
- Copies of any temporary orders or pending motions to discuss
- List of outstanding disclosures and scheduling requests (depositions, expert reports)
- A clear statement of whether you’re open to mediation and any preferred mediators or dates

Typical next steps you may see (general roadmap)
1. Status conference — case management scheduling, discuss settlement/ADR, set deadlines
2. Temporary orders hearing (if requested) — temporary custody, parenting time, support, exclusive possession
3. Discovery period — exchange of financial disclosures, documents, depositions, interrogatories
4. ADR/Mediation — often before trial; many family courts encourage or require it
5. Custody evaluation or Guardian ad Litem (if court orders) — can take weeks to months
6. Pretrial motions and hearings — hearings on evidence, admissibility, or interim disputes
7. Trial or settlement — final orders for custody and support; entry of judgment and decree
8. Post-judgment modifications (future changes in circumstances)

Questions about your rights to raise with your attorney
- My parental rights: what can the other parent request and what can I request?
- My right to parenting time and how supervised or restricted parenting might be imposed
- My right to request/oppose relocation with the child
- My right to seek or oppose temporary orders for support, housing, and custody
- Privacy and discovery rights (what can be compelled and what is protected)
- How to protect the child’s best interests in evidence/presentations

Practical tips for the next two months
- Set up a secure folder (digital and physical) for all case documents and date-stamped evidence
- Start a contemporaneous parenting log (dates, times, activities, missed visits, concerns)
- Gather pay stubs and three years of tax returns for support calculations
- Avoid posting case details on social media
- Keep interactions with the other parent documented and civil — consider communicating via email/text so there’s a record
- Ask your attorney about parenting education classes — some jurisdictions require them

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a concise email or agenda for your meeting with Robert Kim that lists the questions above
- Create a one-page status conference summary you can file/bring to court
- Build a checklist of documents tailored to Hennepin County or Denver once you confirm the correct jurisdiction

Which of those would you like me to do next — and please confirm the correct court (Denver or Hennepin County)? Also tell me the children’s ages and whether there are any immediate safety concerns (domestic violence, threats, relocation plans).

