  - Any police reports, restraining orders, or healthcare documentation if safety/abuse is an issue
- Witnesses
  - Names and contact info for people who can testify about parenting, home environment, or finances (teachers, coaches, neighbors, family)

What to prepare for the status conference
- Short written summary for the judge (often 1–2 pages): current situation, issues in dispute, what relief you’re asking for, what you want to accomplish next
- Proposed timeline/order that you want the court to set (deadlines for discovery, mediation, custody evaluations)
- Copies of any temporary orders or pending motions to discuss
- List of outstanding disclosures and scheduling requests (depositions, expert reports)
- A clear statement of whether you’re open to mediation and any preferred mediators or dates

Typical next steps you may see (general roadmap)
1. Status conference — case management scheduling, discuss settlement/ADR, set deadlines
2. Temporary orders hearing (if requested) — temporary custody, parenting time, support, exclusive possession
3. Discovery period — exchange of financial disclosures, documents, depositions, interrogatories
4. ADR/Mediation — often before trial; many family courts encourage or require it
5. Custody evaluation or Guardian ad Litem (if court orders) — can take weeks to months
6. Pretrial motions and hearings — hearings on evidence, admissibility, or interim disputes
7. Trial or settlement — final orders for custody and support; entry of judgment and decree
8. Post-judgment modifications (future changes in circumstances)

Questions about your rights to raise with your attorney
- My parental rights: what can the other parent request and what can I request?
- My right to parenting time and how supervised or restricted parenting might be imposed
- My right to request/oppose relocation with the child
- My right to seek or oppose temporary orders for support, housing, and custody
- Privacy and discovery rights (what can be compelled and what is protected)
- How to protect the child’s best interests in evidence/presentations

Practical tips for the next two months
- Set up a secure folder (digital and physical) for all case documents and date-stamped evidence
- Start a contemporaneous parenting log (dates, times, activities, missed visits, concerns)
- Gather pay stubs and three years of tax returns for support calculations
- Avoid posting case details on social media
- Keep interactions with the other parent documented and civil — consider communicating via email/text so there’s a record
