Below is a structured plan you can use at your meeting with your attorney and at the status conference.

1) Key questions to ask your attorney (prioritize the ones that matter most to you)
- Jurisdiction & case coordination
  - Which court has proper jurisdiction if there are proceedings in both Denver and Hennepin County? How will the two matters be coordinated?
- Case status & immediate next steps
  - What is the purpose of the status conference the judge set? What should we be prepared to present or propose at that hearing?
  - What timeline should I expect from now until trial (or until a settlement)? What are the likely next formal events (temporary orders hearing, discovery deadlines, mediation, custody evaluation, trial date)?
- Custody / parenting time
  - What is my legal status (custody type) now? What are my rights regarding custody and parenting time while the case proceeds?
  - What factors will the court consider for custody/parenting time in Hennepin County? How do I best document and demonstrate the child’s best interests?
  - Should we seek temporary parenting-time or custody orders now? If so, how do we request them and on what basis?
- Child support & finances
  - How is child support calculated here? What documents will you need to compute temporary and permanent support (pay stubs, tax returns, benefits)?
  - Are there immediate steps to secure financial support or prevent dissipation of assets?
  - If there are shared assets or a business, what interim financial protections or disclosures should we request?
- Temporary orders & emergency relief
  - Should we ask for temporary orders (custody, child support, spousal maintenance, exclusive use of home, restraining orders, etc.) before the status conference or at it?
  - How long do temporary orders typically last, and how can they be modified?
- Discovery & evidence
  - What discovery should we pursue (financial documents, records, communications)? How do we obtain the other party’s records?
  - Should we preserve specific evidence now (texts, emails, social media, school records, medical records, calendars, photos, witnesses)?
  - Are there recommended steps for documenting parenting time, communication, or any incidents (logs, third-party corroboration)?
- Evaluations, experts & professionals
  - Will a custody evaluation or parenting study likely be ordered? If so, what does that involve and how do we prepare?
  - Should we consider hiring experts (psychologist, financial expert, vocational expert)? When is that appropriate?
- Settlement & alternative dispute resolution
  - What are our settlement options (mediation, collaborative law, negotiation)? Is mediation required or recommended in Hennepin County?
  - What’s the likelihood you recommend settlement vs. litigating to trial given our facts?
- Costs, timing, and communication
  - What are your fees, expected costs (experts, evaluations, filing fees), and billing practices going forward?
  - How will we communicate—email, phone, portal—and what response times should I expect?
- Strategy & my role
  - What are the strongest and weakest points of our case right now?
  - What immediate steps should I take or avoid (relocation, new social media posts, changing insurance, enrolling kids in new school)?
  - What decisions will require my input vs. what you can handle administratively?

2) Documents to gather now (bring to your meeting and have copies for your attorney)
- Court papers: petitions, summons, any existing orders, any filings from the other side
- Identification & contact info: full legal names, addresses, birthdates of children
- Financial documents (last 2–3 years if possible):
  - Pay stubs (last 3–6 months), employer benefits info, tax returns (last 2 years), W-2s/1099s
  - Bank statements, retirement statements, investment accounts, property deeds, mortgage statements
  - Business records if self-employed (profit & loss statements)
- Monthly household budget: expenses, childcare, medical costs, education
- Child-related records: school enrollment, IEPs, report cards, medical records, daycare/childcare costs
- Communication logs: texts, emails, call logs showing parenting time arrangements, any concerning behavior
- Incident documentation: police reports, medical records, photographs, witness names
- Prior agreements: separation agreements, temporary agreements, custody arrangements, parenting plans
- Insurance info: health, dental, life, auto
- Any proof of payments: child support or spousal support already paid, transfers, receipts
- Any legal notices or restraining orders

3) What to prepare for the status conference (2 months out)
- Be ready to propose a case plan: suggested timeline for disclosures, discovery, mediation, custody evaluations, and potential trial dates
- Decide whether to request temporary orders now or if the attorney should attempt to resolve those before the hearing
- Have a concise statement of the main issues (custody, parenting time, child support, temporary financial needs) summarized in bullet points for the judge/attorney
- Prepare to identify any urgent matters requiring immediate relief
- Ask your attorney to file any necessary pre-conference documents (case management plan, proposed scheduling order, affidavits) before the hearing

4) Practical tips for strengthening your position
- Keep calm, consistent records: maintain a chronological parenting log (dates, times, exchanges, missed visits, important conversations)
- Avoid posting about the case or children on social media
- If safety is an issue, document and bring that up ASAP
- Don’t move the child out of state or take any action that could complicate jurisdiction without attorney approval
- Keep copies of all financial transactions, and don’t hide or shift assets

5) Typical case progression (what often happens next; confirm with your attorney)
- Status conference → judge issues scheduling/order
- Exchange of financial disclosures and initial discovery
- Mediation or settlement discussions (often required or encouraged)
- Temporary orders hearing if parties need immediate orders
- Possible custody evaluation or expert reports if contested
- Further discovery, depositions if needed
- Pretrial conference and then trial if no settlement

6) Suggested agenda for your meeting with Robert Kim
- Confirm representation and jurisdiction (Denver vs. Hennepin) — clarify if multiple courts
- Review timeline for status conference and what the court expects
- Go over the prioritized list of immediate goals (temporary orders, support, custody)
- Review documents you’ve collected and what’s still needed
- Decide next filings and a preparation timetable (who will draft what, deadlines)
- Schedule follow-up check-in before the status conference

7) Offer to draft an email or checklist
