I can help you get organized and make the most of your time with Robert Kim. A couple quick clarifying questions before I give a detailed plan and lists:
- Which court is handling this matter — Denver Family Court or Hennepin County (Minneapolis)? You mentioned both; we should confirm jurisdiction because procedure and forms differ.
- Do you already have temporary orders in place for custody/support?  
- Do you want me to draft an email to Robert Kim with these questions and a request to confirm the status-conference date/time and expected filings?

Below is a practical checklist you can use to prepare questions, gather paperwork, and plan next steps before the status conference.

1) Immediate clarifying questions for your attorney
- Confirm jurisdiction and venue (Denver vs Hennepin County) and the judge assigned to the case.
- Confirm the exact date/time/location of the status conference and whether it will be in-person or remote.
- What is the courtroom procedure for a status conference in this county (what the judge typically expects)?
- Who will attend the status conference (attorneys only, clients, mediator)? Will a calendaring order or scheduling order be issued?
- What filings or disclosures does the court expect before the status conference (financial affidavits, parenting plan proposals, witness lists)?
- Are there any immediate temporary orders we should seek or modify (custody/parenting time, child support, spousal support, exclusive use of the home, restraining orders)?
- Do we need an emergency hearing or temporary relief before the status conference?
- What discovery will we exchange and on what timeline (documents, interrogatories, depositions)?
- Will the court order mediation, parenting evaluation, or custody study? Who pays?
- What is the strategy for custody (joint/legal custody, primary physical custody, supervised visits, a phased parenting plan)?
- How is child support calculated here — do we use state guidelines and what inputs are needed?
- What financial documents do you need from me (pay stubs, tax returns, bank statements, investment statements, mortgage, debts, retirement)?
- Are spousal-support issues likely? What factors and evidence will influence that?
- What evidence should I preserve now (texts, emails, calendars, school/medical records, police reports)?
- Any high-risk issues we should address (domestic violence, substance abuse, relocation risk, safety concerns for the child)?
- Cost and timeline expectations: likely schedule to trial if no settlement, and estimated attorney fees and costs for next steps (mediation, experts).
- Communication protocol: how and when should I communicate with you about requests/offers from the other side?
- Settlement posture: are there key items we should attempt to resolve now vs later?
- If the other parent moves or changes employment, how will that affect custody/support?
- What are the likely next hearings and deadlines after the status conference?

2) Documents to gather (start now)
- Photo ID and full contact info for all parties
- Court papers you’ve received (complaint, petitions, temporary orders, notices)
- Pay stubs (last 3 months) and year-to-date earnings
- Federal and state tax returns (past 2–3 years)
- Bank statements (past 6–12 months)
- Credit card statements, loan/mortgage statements
- Retirement and investment account statements
- Recent bills and household expenses
- Child-related expenses (daycare, medical, school, extracurriculars)
- Receipts for significant one-time expenses
- Parenting-time records (calendars showing pickup/dropoff, missed visits)
- Communications: texts, emails, social media posts relevant to custody or parenting
- School records, medical records for the children, special needs documentation
- Police reports, protection orders, witness contact info (if safety issues exist)
- Any pre/post-nup or separation agreements, if applicable

3) What typically happens at a status conference (so you know what to expect)
- The judge or clerk confirms the case schedule and outstanding discovery deadlines
- The court may set deadlines for disclosure and exchange of financial information
- The court often orders mediation or a parenting evaluation if custody is contested
- The judge may enter temporary orders or set a date for a temporary hearing
- The judge will set a trial date window (or a schedule leading to trial) or encourage settlement steps
- The judge may ask parties if they’ve exchanged proposed parenting plans or child support worksheets

4) Practical next steps and timeline (two-month window to status conference)
- Immediately: confirm jurisdiction/date, start document collection, preserve evidence (don’t delete messages), create a chronology of key events.
- Within 1–2 weeks: provide financial docs to attorney; prepare a proposed parenting plan or at least a summary of your parenting time request; identify witnesses and expert needs.
- 2–4 weeks: exchange financial affidavits if required; attempt early mediation or settlement talks if advised; consider requesting temporary orders if current arrangements aren’t working.
- 4–6 weeks (before status conference): draft or review motions you need filed, finalize exhibits, and prepare a short memo of objectives for the status conference to give to your lawyer.
- Status conference: attend (in person or remotely), be prepared to answer basic scheduling and settlement-prep questions.
- After status conference: follow the docket/scheduling order, complete discovery, engage in ordered mediation/evaluation, prepare for dispositive hearings or trial as scheduled.

5) Questions about your rights (what to specifically ask about)
- What are my parental rights regarding decision-making (education, medical, religion)?
- What are my rights to parenting time/visitation and what are typical schedules in our county?
- Can the other parent relocate with the children — what notice is required and what process is used?
- What protections exist if there are safety concerns (restraining order, supervised parenting time)?
- What is the process to modify temporary orders later if circumstances change?
- What privacy and financial protections do I have (protecting accounts, preventing unilateral selling of assets)?
- What are my options if the other parent doesn’t follow a court order?

6) Evidence & preparation tips
- Keep a single folder (digital or physical) for all case documents and label them clearly.
- Maintain a simple timeline (chronology) of events with dates and brief notes.
- Save and back up all communications with the other parent.
- Avoid posting anything about the case or the children on social media.
- Limit direct communication with the other parent to logistics; consider text/email only so there’s a record.
- If there are safety issues, document incidents contemporaneously and report to your lawyer immediately.

7) Settlement and alternative dispute resolution
- Ask your attorney about mediation, collaborative law, parenting coordination, and costs for evaluations.
- Discuss what your “best” and “bottom-line” settlement positions are for custody and support.
- Ask what a realistic settlement looks like in your county based on facts similar to yours.

8) Cost management and experts
- Ask about potential expert costs (custody evaluators, child psychologists, forensic accountants) and whether we need them now or only if the case escalates.
- Discuss payment plans, retainer, and how costs are billed.

9) If you want, I can:
- Draft an organized email to Robert Kim listing these questions and asking for confirmation of jurisdiction/status conference details.
