Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — this is a lot to manage, and it’s smart to go into the status conference organized. A few quick clarifying questions before I draft anything for you or make a checklist you can use with Robert Kim:

- Which court has the active case: Family Court in Denver or Hennepin County (Minneapolis)? (You mentioned both.) The answers and local procedures vary by jurisdiction, so I’ll tailor recommendations once you confirm.
- Do you already have any temporary orders in place (custody/parenting time, child support, protection orders)?
- Do you want a short email I can draft to Robert Kim asking these questions + requesting next steps, or would you prefer a checklist to bring to your meeting?

Below is a focused, practical set of things to ask your attorney and the typical next steps and documents you should assemble.

Questions to ask your attorney (grouped and prioritized)
- Case basics & immediate next steps
  - Who will attend the status conference? (You, opposing counsel, judge, magistrate)
  - What is the purpose of the status conference in this court (scheduling, temporary orders, mediation referral)?
  - What should I have ready for that conference (documents, proposals, witnesses)?
  - Are there immediate safety or emergency steps I should consider (temporary orders, restraining orders)?

- Jurisdiction & service
  - Is this court the correct forum for all issues (custody/support) given where the child and parties live?
  - Has the opposing party been properly served and is service complete?

- Custody (parenting time / decision-making)
  - How does this court define legal custody vs. physical custody/parenting time, and what can I realistically expect?
  - What factors will the court consider here (child’s best interest factors specific to this state/county)?
  - Do I have rights to emergency or temporary parenting time orders while the case proceeds?
  - If relocation becomes an issue, what rules apply and how is relocation decided?
  - What is the process for parenting evaluations, custody investigations, or guardian ad litem appointment in this jurisdiction — when and how are they ordered and what do they cost?

- Child support & financial issues
  - How is child support calculated here (guidelines/formulas) and what documents will be used?
  - Can temporary child support be ordered now? If so, on what basis?
  - What about spousal support/alimony — are there preliminary orders or disclosures required?

- Discovery, disclosures, evidence
  - What written disclosures/financial affidavits are required and on what deadline?
  - What discovery tools will we likely use (interrogatories, document requests, depositions)?
  - What evidence will be most important for custody (school records, medical, communications, witnesses, CPS reports)?
  - Should I start keeping a parenting log, and if so, what details are most useful?

- Settlement and alternative dispute resolution
  - Does this court require mediation or parenting education before trial?
  - What settlement options do you recommend and what’s the negotiation strategy?
  - If mediation is recommended, will the mediator have authority to recommend a parenting plan?

- Timeline, costs, and trial
  - Based on the court’s typical schedule, what is a realistic timeline to resolution if we litigate vs settle?
  - What are likely fees and costs (your fees, expert evaluations, mediation, GAL)?
  - If we go to trial, what is the expected length, and when might it be scheduled?

- Strategy & decision points
  - What are my strongest arguments and main risks in custody and support?
  - What documents or witnesses would be most helpful for our position?
  - What short-term accommodations should I request (temporary parenting plan/support) at the status conference?

- Enforcement and modifications
  - How are orders enforced if the other parent doesn’t comply?
  - What is the process for modifying custody/support later if circumstances change?

Documents and materials to gather now
- Child-related: birth certificate, school records, IEPs (if any), medical/mental health records, daycare records, immunization records, scheduling logs.
- Communications: relevant text messages, emails, social media, voicemail summaries, records of in-person exchanges showing custody/parenting patterns.
- Legal & safety: any police reports, protection orders, CPS reports, prior court orders (if any).
- Financial: recent paystubs, last 2–3 years of tax returns, bank statements, investment statements, mortgage/rent, credit card statements, retirement account statements, proof of health insurance and children’s expenses.
- Parenting evidence: a calendar or log of parenting time, unusual incidents, missed visits, or other issues.
- Anything showing child’s routine and ties to community (school activities, doctors, extended family).

What a “status conference” usually looks like and typical next steps
- Status conference purpose: judge or magistrate checks case status, sets deadlines, may issue scheduling orders, and may encourage/require mediation or settlement discussions.
- Common immediate orders: scheduling deadlines (discovery cutoff, expert witness disclosure), requirement for financial disclosures, referral to mediation or parenting classes, possible temporary orders (if requested and supported).
- Next steps after the status conference: exchange required disclosures, conduct discovery, complete mediation or parent education, obtain any requested evaluations (custody/parenting or psychological), file pretrial motions or settlement if agreed.
- If no settlement: pretrial conference(s), final pretrial order, then trial. Trials and evaluations can add months depending on court backlog.

Practical preparation checklist for the next 2 months (before the status conference and right after)
- Confirm and correct jurisdiction/court location with your attorney.
- Prepare a one-page timeline of key events (incidents, exchanges, dates).
- Assemble the document packet above (digital copies preferred): financials, school/medical, communications.
- Draft a proposed temporary parenting plan and a proposed support estimate (ask attorney for help/calculator).
- Start a daily parenting log (date/time, exchanges, missed times, important events).
- Ask your attorney about experts (custody evaluator, mental health) and budget for them if needed.
- Decide your priorities (what you must get vs. what you can compromise on).
- Schedule a meeting with Robert Kim at least 1–2 weeks before the status conference to finalize position and any temporary orders you'll request.

Sample short email you can send to your attorney (I can draft/send if you want)
Subject: Preparation for status conference — questions and documents

Hi Robert — Our status conference is in two months. I want to be fully prepared. Could we review the following at our next call/meeting?
- Purpose of the status conference in this court and what we should bring.
- Any temporary orders we should seek beforehand (custody/support).
- Required disclosures and deadlines I should prepare for (financial affidavits, parenting evaluations).
- Suggested documents to gather (I can bring digital copies).
- Whether mediation or a custody evaluation is likely and the expected timeline/cost.
Also, please let me know a convenient time to meet 1–2 weeks before the conference. Thank you. — [Your name]

Safety and immediate issues
- If there are any safety concerns for you or the child (threats, violence, child endangerment), tell your attorney immediately — emergency/temporary orders can often be requested right away.
- If the other parent is likely to remove the child from the jurisdiction, ask about immediate relief and emergency motions.

Next step from me
- Confirm which court (Denver or Hennepin County) and whether you want: 1) a polished email to Robert Kim I will draft (you can review and send), or 2) a printable 1–2 page checklist to bring to your meeting, or 3) both.
- If you confirm the court, I can tailor questions and timeline items to that jurisdiction’s typical rules.

I’m ready to draft the email or create the checklist when you confirm the court and whether you want the message sent or saved as a draft.

