Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I’m not a lawyer, but I can help you organize questions and next steps to discuss with Robert Kim so you’re prepared for the status conference and the months ahead. A couple quick clarifying points before the list:
- You mentioned both “family court in Denver” and “Hennepin County, Minnesota.” Which court actually has the case? Jurisdiction matters for procedures and timelines — confirm that with your attorney if you’re unsure.
- This is general guidance; your attorney should confirm how it applies to your case.

What to ask your attorney — organized by topic

Case basics & jurisdiction
- Which court has jurisdiction (Denver or Hennepin County)? Does that affect timeline or applicable laws?
- What is the current status of the case (what’s already filed, what disclosures have been made)?
- Who will attend the status conference, and what should I expect to happen there?
- What is our goal going into the status conference (scheduling order, temporary orders, mediation referral)?

Custody (legal vs. physical) & parenting time
- What types of custody orders are possible under the applicable law (legal decision-making, physical custody/parenting time)?
- What are my rights regarding decision-making for education, health care, religion, extracurricular activities?
- Do you recommend proposing a temporary parenting plan before the status conference?
- If I’m concerned about safety, can we request supervised visitation or no-contact orders?
- What happens if the other parent wants to relocate with the child?

Child support & financial support
- How is child support calculated in this jurisdiction? What documents do you need to run a support calculation?
- If I need temporary child support or spousal support, how do we request it and how quickly can it be granted?
- What financial documents should I gather now (pay stubs, tax returns, bank statements, debts, daycare costs, medical expenses)?

Temporary/emergency orders
- Should we seek temporary orders now (custody, support, exclusive use of the home, restraining orders)?
- What is the standard for emergency relief here, and how quickly can the court act?
- What evidence is needed to support a temporary order motion?

Discovery and evidence
- What discovery should we request (financial disclosures, communications, phone records, school records, medical records)?
- How do we preserve evidence (texts, emails, social media, photos, recordings)? Any chain-of-custody concerns?
- Do we need affidavits from witnesses (family, teachers, counselors)?
- Should we ask the court for subpoenas if the other party won’t produce records?

Evaluations, experts, and professionals
- Will the court likely order a custody evaluation or appoint a guardian ad litem (GAL)? If so, what is the process and timeline?
- Should we proactively seek evaluations (psychologist, parenting coordinator, drug testing)?
- Who pays for court-ordered evaluations or experts? Can we request the court split costs or appoint an evaluator we propose?

Mediation, settlement, and ADR
- Is mediation or parenting coordination mandatory here? When does it typically occur?
- How do settlement negotiations proceed? What are realistic settlement options to consider?
- If mediation fails, what are the next steps toward trial?

Timeline & process (what to expect after status conference)
- What deadlines will likely be set at the status conference (discovery cutoff, expert disclosures, motion deadlines, trial date)?
- Typical timeline for a custody/support case here (months to a year+ depending on complexity).
- How long does it usually take to get a trial date? What factors speed things up or slow them down?

Your role & evidence to prepare now
- What documents should I gather before the status conference? (See checklist below.)
- What should I be tracking now — calendar of parenting exchanges/visits, missed visits, expenses, communications, incidents?
- How should I communicate with the other parent (avoid hostile texts; keep records)?

Costs & fees
- What are the anticipated costs (attorney fees, filing fees, expert fees, evaluator fees)?
- What are options if I can’t afford certain costs (fee waivers, payment plans, requests for the other party to pay)?

Trial & hearing preparation
- What will trial look like here (length, witnesses, evidence rules)?
- How do we prepare witnesses and exhibits?
- When do we exchange witness and exhibit lists?

Enforcement & modifications
- How is enforcement handled if the other parent doesn’t follow orders?
- How do modifications work later (relocation, changes in income, change in child’s needs)?

Risks & strategy
- What are the strengths and weaknesses of my position?
- What are the realistic outcomes we should prepare for?
- What is your recommended strategy (settle, litigate, seek specific temporary orders)?

Administrative / practical
- Who is my main contact at your office? Best way to reach them and expected response times?
- Are there local forms or procedures I need to complete before the conference?
- Do you need anything from me before an initial meeting (signed retainer, ID, financial forms)?

Suggested documents and items to bring to your next meeting with Robert Kim
- Chronological incident summary / timeline (dates, short descriptions)
- Parenting schedule you want (proposed parenting plan)
- Pay stubs (last 3–6 months) and W-2 / tax returns (last 2 years)
- Bank statements, retirement/asset statements, mortgage/rent, credit card/debt statements
- Child care, medical, school, and daycare expenses/receipts
- School records, IEPs, medical records for the child
- Police reports, protective orders, or other safety-related documents (if applicable)
- Copies of important communications (texts, emails) — export and save them as PDF if possible
- Witness names and contact info (teachers, pediatricians, family members)
- Any prior court orders, pleadings, or custody agreements

What the status conference will usually accomplish
- The judge commonly issues a scheduling order (deadlines for discovery, motions, evaluations)
- The judge may encourage or order mediation or evaluations
- The conference is usually procedural — not a full evidentiary hearing — but it sets the roadmap for the case

Next actions I can help you with
- Draft a concise email or meeting agenda to send to Robert Kim listing these questions
- Create a checklist or timeline template to track visits, expenses, and communications
- Prepare a proposed parenting plan or sample temporary order language to discuss with your attorney

Would you like me to draft a short email or meeting agenda you can send to Robert Kim that includes the most critical questions and the documents you’ll bring? Also please confirm which court (Denver or Hennepin County) is handling the case so I can tailor the checklist and timeline to the correct jurisdiction.

