Assistant: I can help — this is a good time to get organized. First, one quick clarification: you mentioned “family court in Denver” and also “Hennepin County, Minnesota.” Which court is actually handling this case? The procedural steps and local rules differ by jurisdiction, so confirm that and I’ll tailor anything I draft to the right court. 

Below is a practical checklist: what to ask your attorney (grouped by topic), what documents to gather, and a typical timeline/next steps so you’ll be ready for the status conference and the months after.

Questions to ask your attorney (copyable, grouped)
- Case basics and strategy
  - What is our litigation strategy and goals for custody, parenting time, and support?
  - What outcome do you view as realistic given my circumstances?
  - What deadlines should I be aware of between now and the status conference?

- Jurisdiction / venue (confirm now)
  - Which court has jurisdiction (Denver/Colorado or Hennepin County/Minnesota)? Does jurisdiction raise any immediate issues?
  - Are there any local rules I should know about for status conferences and hearings?

- Status conference specifics
  - What is the purpose of the status conference in our case?
  - Will the judge expect a prepared status report, proposed scheduling order, or parenting plan?
  - Should I appear in person or by phone/video? What should I bring?

- Temporary orders (custody/relief while case proceeds)
  - Do we need temporary custody, parenting time, or child support orders before final resolution?
  - If we need temporary relief, how do we request it and what evidence is required?
  - What can I do now to strengthen a request (or defend against one)?

- Custody / parenting time (best interest issues)
  - How does the court determine custody/parenting time here (what standard/factors are used)?
  - What evidence matters most (school records, communications, witness testimony, evaluations)?
  - Should we propose a parenting plan now? What should it include?

- Child support and finances
  - How will child support be calculated? What documents do you need (pay stubs, tax returns)?
  - Are there temporary support guidelines or emergency support we should seek?
  - If there’s disagreement about income (self-employment, bonuses), how do we handle it?

- Discovery and evidence
  - What discovery should we start now (financial disclosures, parenting time logs, emails/texts)?
  - Will we need subpoenas, forensic records, or expert evaluations (e.g., custody evaluator, accountant)?
  - How should I preserve evidence and communications (best practices for saving texts, social media, calendars)?

- Alternative dispute resolution / settlement
  - Is mediation or collaborative law required or recommended here?
  - What are the timing and cost implications of mediation versus going to trial?
  - At what point should we consider settlement negotiations?

- Safety / special concerns
  - If there are safety concerns, domestic violence, substance abuse, or emergency issues, how do we address them immediately?
  - Are there protective orders or supervised parenting time options we should pursue?

- Costs, timeline, and next steps
  - What is the estimated timeline from status conference to resolution (typical time to trial or settlement here)?
  - What are likely costs (fees, experts, evaluations), and can anything be minimized?
  - What are the next immediate steps I should take in the next 2 months?

Documents to gather now
- Identification and case documents: court papers, summons, prior orders, any prior custody agreements
- Financials: most recent 2–3 years’ tax returns, last 3–6 months of pay stubs, W-2s/1099s, bank statements, retirement statements, mortgage/lease, credit card statements, loan documents
- Income specifics: child support or spousal support received/paid, business profit & loss (if self-employed)
- Child-related: school records, IEPs, medical records, vaccination records, daycare details and receipts
- Communication history: message logs, texts, emails, parenting calendars (document dates/times of exchanges, missed visits)
- Safety records: police reports, protection orders, CPS reports, medical records
- Witness list and contact details (relatives, teachers, childcare providers who can speak to child’s routine or needs)

What to prepare for the status conference
- A short chronology of the case and major events (chronological bullet list)
- A proposed parenting plan or at least the key terms you want (schedules, holiday plan, exchanges)
- A list of outstanding issues you want the court to address (temporary orders, discovery deadlines, mediation)
- Copies of any outstanding emergency or temporary orders you’ve been part of
- Questions you want the judge to consider (present to your attorney so they can raise them)

Typical next-step timeline (general guide)
- Status conference (now scheduled in ~2 months): judge will often set a schedule, and may order mediation or parenting evaluation or set dates for discovery and pretrial motions
- Scheduling order (within weeks of status conference): deadlines for exchanging documents, expert disclosures, and mediation
- Discovery and ADR (2–4 months): exchange financial info, depositions, and mediation/settlement talks; sometimes parenting evaluations happen here
- Motions / hearings (as needed): temporary orders or motions about discovery, custody, or support
- Trial (if not settled): could be 6–12+ months from start depending on court backlog and complexity
Note: timelines vary widely by jurisdiction and complexity; your attorney can give a more precise estimate.

Practical tips and immediate actions for you
- Start a parenting calendar now (dates/times of exchanges, missed visits, important incidents). Keep it factual and concise.
- Save all financial documents and communications in a folder (digital + backup).
- Avoid heated text exchanges; keep communications civil and document important agreements in writing.
- Ask your attorney if there’s any written intake or form you should complete for the status conference (financial affidavits, proposed scheduling orders).

Safety / emergency note
- If you or your child are in immediate danger, contact local law enforcement or a domestic violence hotline right away. Let your attorney know immediately if safety issues exist.

Would you like me to:
- Draft a concise email to Robert Kim listing these questions and asking for specific instructions before the status conference? (I can draft it and you can review before sending.)
- Or customize this checklist to the correct jurisdiction once you confirm whether the case is in Denver (Colorado) or Hennepin County (Minnesota)?

