Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can help you get organized and ready. Quick clarification before I go deeper: you mentioned a family court in Denver and also Hennepin County, Minnesota. Which court is this actually filed in? The jurisdiction matters for specific procedures and deadlines. Also, do you want me to draft an email or question list to send to your attorney Robert Kim (I can do that if you want)?

Below is a practical checklist of what to ask your attorney and what to expect next. This is general guidance (not legal advice); your attorney will confirm the law and procedures that apply to your specific jurisdiction.

High-level items to confirm with your attorney
- Confirm the correct court and case number (Denver family court vs Hennepin County) and which judge is assigned.
- Confirm the status conference date and what the judge expects at that hearing.
- Ask about immediate deadlines and any temporary orders currently in place (custody, child support, stay-away, possession of property).

Specific questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic)

Case basics and strategy
- What is our overall strategy for custody and support? What are our goals and likely outcomes?
- What are my parental rights under the applicable law?
- What are the biggest legal risks or weaknesses in my position?
- How long do you expect this case might take to resolve (best case / typical / worst case)?

Custody and parenting time
- What custody arrangement will the court consider in my jurisdiction (legal custody, physical custody, parenting time)?
- How is the child's best interest evaluated here? What factors matter most?
- Do we need a custody evaluation or guardian ad litem? If so, what does that process look like and how long will it take?
- Can we ask for temporary parenting orders before final resolution? Should we?

Child support and financial issues
- How is child support calculated here? What financial documents will be required?
- Are there temporary child support or spousal support orders we should request now?
- If my income is variable, how will that be handled?
- How do health insurance, childcare, and tax dependency get handled?

Discovery, evidence, and documents
- What documents should I gather now? (See checklist below.)
- Will we need depositions, expert reports, or forensic accounting?
- How should we handle digital evidence (texts, emails, social media)? Should I preserve everything?
- What about witnesses — who should we interview or list?

Temporary/emergency orders
- Should we seek temporary orders (custody, support, residence, restraining)? If so, what’s the process and timeline?
- What qualifies as an emergency that would warrant an immediate hearing?

Mediation, settlement, and alternatives
- Is mediation or mandatory settlement conference required in this court?
- What is the likely cost-benefit of settlement vs trial in my case?
- What settlement terms would be acceptable to me (parenting schedule, support, property)?

Court process & timeline
- What usually happens at the status conference? (Commonly: case scheduling, deadlines for discovery, referral to mediation/evaluation.)
- What are the next procedural steps after the status conference? (e.g., exchange financial affidavits, mediation, custody evaluation, pretrial)
- How long between status conference and trial in typical cases in this court?

Costs, fees, and communication
- What is your estimate of legal fees and other costs (experts, evaluations)?
- How will you bill (hourly, retainer)? What is the retainer amount?
- Who will be my point of contact in your office and how do I reach you about urgent matters?
- How will decisions be made — do you need my approval for settlements below a certain threshold?

Practical & safety concerns
- If there are safety or domestic violence concerns, how do we raise them and get protection?
- If the other parent wants to relocate with the child, what process applies?

What to bring to your next meeting with the attorney (documents checklist)
- Court papers you have (complaint, summons, any orders, notices, status conference notice)
- Financial documents for you and the other parent (recent pay stubs, tax returns last 2–3 years, W-2s, 1099s, bank statements, retirement statements)
- Proof of monthly household expenses (mortgage/rent, utilities, insurance, tuition, childcare)
- Child-related documents (birth certificate, school records, medical records, daycare invoices)
- Records of parenting time (calendar of who had the child and when), missed visits, and who cares for the child
- Communications with the other parent relevant to custody/support (texts, emails, messages)
- Any evidence of safety concerns (police reports, photos, medical records)
- Names and contact info for witnesses (family, childcare providers, teachers)
- Any prenuptial/postnuptial agreements or prior court orders
- Social media posts or public statements relevant to the case (preserve screenshots)

Immediate practical steps you can take now
- Calendar the status conference and any deadlines.
- Start gathering the documents listed above.
- Preserve communications and avoid deleting messages or posts.
- Limit social media posting about the case or the other parent.
- Avoid making any major unilateral decisions about relocating the child, changing insurance, or moving out (discuss with your attorney first).
- If immediate danger exists, call local law enforcement and tell your attorney.

Typical next-step timeline (general)
- Status conference (in 1–2 months, as scheduled) — judge sets deadlines/scheduling.
- Short-term/temporary orders (weeks to months) — if requested, the court may hold a temporary hearing.
- Discovery and financial disclosures (1–3 months after status conference, can be longer).
- Mediation/settlement negotiations (often required before trial; timing varies).
- Custody evaluation/guardian ad litem (if ordered; can take several months).
- Pretrial conference and trial (can be 6+ months to over a year depending on court docket and complexity).

Questions to ask yourself before meeting your attorney
- What are my top priorities (primary custody, shared custody, financial stability, quick resolution)?
- What compromises am I willing to make?
- What are my non-negotiables?
- Are there any urgent safety or logistical needs for the child?

Would you like me to:
- Draft an email or a concise list of questions to send to Robert Kim? (I can draft it for you to review.)
- Search your inbox for communications from Robert Kim or the court (I can look for emails from Kim & Associates or the court if you want me to)?
- Create a printable checklist/one-page plan for your next steps?

Tell me which court is correct (Denver or Hennepin County) and whether you want me to draft that email/question list to your attorney now.

