I can help with that. First — quick clarification: you mentioned proceedings at family court in Denver and also Hennepin County, Minnesota. Which court is handling this case (Denver or Hennepin County)? Also, do you already have the status conference date from the judge? Once I have that I can tailor the checklist and any email to your attorney.

Below is a structured list you can use to (A) know what to ask your attorney, (B) gather materials, and (C) understand typical next steps so you’ll be ready for the status conference and the months after.

A. Key questions to ask your attorney (prioritize the top 8–10 at the first meeting)
- Jurisdiction & case basics
  - Which court has jurisdiction (confirm Denver vs. Hennepin County) and does that affect strategy?
  - What deadlines and important dates should I be aware of (status conference date, discovery deadlines, hearing/trial dates)?
- Status conference specifics
  - What will happen at the status conference? What will the judge expect from us?
  - What should I bring prepared (proposed schedule, parenting plan, financial estimates)?
- Custody/parenting time
  - What are my parental rights right now? Are there temporary custody or parenting-time orders I should seek or modify?
  - What factors will the court consider for custody/parenting time in this jurisdiction?
  - Should we propose a temporary parenting plan before the status conference? If so, what should it include?
- Child support and finances
  - How will child support be calculated here? What financial documents will the court require?
  - Should we seek temporary child support or temporary spousal support orders now?
- Safety, emergency, and protective issues
  - Do we need an emergency/temporary order (e.g., protective/ restraining/ no-contact) before the status conference?
  - If there are safety concerns, what immediate steps should I take?
- Evidence, discovery, and documentation
  - What evidence and documents should I be gathering now? (See checklist below)
  - Will we need to serve discovery requests? What is the typical discovery timeline?
- Experts and evaluations
  - Will a custody evaluation, guardian ad litem, or mental health expert likely be ordered or recommended?
  - If so, how do we prepare and do we need our own experts?
- Communication and settlement strategy
  - What are realistic outcomes or settlement options?
  - Would mediation or alternative dispute resolution be recommended here and at what stage?
- Fees and logistics
  - What are anticipated legal costs and costs for experts/ evaluations?
  - How often should we meet/communicate and what’s the best way to communicate sensitive info?
- Next tactical steps
  - What should I (personally) do between now and the status conference to strengthen our case or protect my rights?
  - Are there documents or motions you recommend filing before the status conference?

B. Documents and information to gather now
- Identification and court paperwork:
  - Copies of any court filings you’ve received or filed, the status conference notice, and any temporary orders.
- Financial documents (past 2–3 years where possible):
  - Pay stubs, tax returns (1040s), W-2s, 1099s
  - Bank statements, investment account statements, retirement account statements
  - Mortgage or lease documents, property deeds/ titles, vehicle loans
  - Credit card statements, debt records
  - Business income/expense records if self-employed
- Child-related records:
  - School records, IEPs, counselor/therapy notes, medical records (major illnesses, mental health), vaccination records
  - Childcare costs, extracurricular costs
- Communication and behavioral records:
  - Texts, emails, social media messages relevant to custody/support or parenting
  - Calendars or logs of parenting time, missed visits, or concerns
  - Any police reports, protective order filings, or incident reports
- Other evidence:
  - Photographs, video, witness contact info (if relevant)
  - Documentation of household contributions (who pays utilities, childcare, etc.)
- Expenses documentation if seeking temporary support:
  - Rent/mortgage, utilities, groceries, medical expenses, childcare receipts

C. What typically happens next in family court cases (general timeline)
- Initial status conference: judge sets scheduling deadlines, may encourage settlement or set dates for mediation/evaluations, addresses temporary orders.
- Temporary orders (if needed): either party can request temporary custody/parenting time/support; the judge may issue temporary orders pending final resolution.
- Discovery: exchange of financial info, written questions (interrogatories), document production, depositions if needed.
- Mediation/conciliation: many courts require or encourage mediation to attempt resolution on custody/support prior to trial.
- Evaluations/experts (if ordered): custody evaluations, mental health assessments, or expert testimony scheduling and preparation.
- Pretrial conference & motions: hearings on specific motions (e.g., to compel discovery, emergency relief) and pretrial scheduling.
- Trial or settlement: if not resolved, case goes to trial where a judge issues final orders on custody/support/assets.
Note: The exact order and requirements vary by jurisdiction and judge.

D. Practical preparations before the status conference
- Draft a proposed temporary parenting schedule and basic parenting plan (times, holidays, decision-making).
- Prepare a short written summary for your attorney highlighting the most important facts (safety issues, major conflicts, financial concerns).
