I can help. Below is a practical checklist of what to ask your attorney, what to prepare and bring, and the typical next steps and timeline in custody/support proceedings. Before we go further: you mentioned both Denver and Hennepin County, MN. Which court is actually handling the case? That affects some deadlines/procedures — please confirm the correct county and the status-conference date if you know it.

Questions to ask your attorney (priority order)
- Jurisdiction & venue
  - Which court is handling this matter (Hennepin County or Denver)? Is the court’s jurisdiction certain?
  - Are there any jurisdictional issues we need to address now (other pending cases, out-of-state moves, jurisdictional objections)?
- Status conference specifics
  - What is the purpose of the status conference in our case and what does the judge typically expect?
  - What documents or proposals should I bring to that conference?
  - Will the judge set a schedule (deadlines, mediation, trial date) at the status conference?
- Immediate protections and temporary orders
  - Do we need temporary orders now (custody/parenting time, child support, spousal support, exclusive use of the home, temporary restraining orders)?
  - If temporary orders are needed, what is the process and likely timeline?
- Custody (legal and physical)
  - What custody outcome should I aim for, and what factors will the court consider?
  - Would the court likely appoint a custody evaluator or GAL (guardian ad litem)? How long does that take and what does it cost?
  - What evidence will help establish my parenting position (communication logs, witnesses, school/medical records)?
- Child support / financial issues
  - How is child support calculated in this jurisdiction? What documents do you need to prepare accurate calculations (pay stubs, tax returns)?
  - Should we request temporary child support now?
  - If the other party is self-employed or has unusual income, how do we handle imputed income or business records?
- Discovery & evidence
  - What discovery will we request (financial documents, emails/texts, social media, records)?
  - Should we preserve and gather specific documents now (phone logs, text messages, photos, travel receipts)?
- Alternative dispute resolution and settlement
  - Is mediation or settlement conference required or likely? When would that happen?
  - What’s your negotiation strategy and settlement thresholds?
- Timeline & next major dates
  - What are the anticipated next steps and typical timeline from status conference to resolution or trial?
  - When should I expect to receive and produce requested documents?
- Costs & billing
  - Estimated costs for each stage (mediation, custody evaluation, discovery, trial).
  - How do you bill and how often will you update me on costs?
- Communications & roles
  - How should I communicate with you (email/phone) and your expected response times?
  - Will anyone else from the firm be handling parts of my case?
- Risks & outcomes
  - What are the best-case and worst-case outcomes and the likelihood of each?
  - Are there immediate actions I should or should not take (relocation, travel with the child, changing school or medical providers)?
- Next immediate steps
  - What should I do in the next week (documents to gather, statements to prepare, behavior to avoid)?

Documents and records to gather now
- Identification of the children (birth certificates), custody orders from any prior cases
- Any existing court filings and orders (complaints, petitions, temporary orders)
- Last 3 years of federal tax returns (all schedules)
- Recent pay stubs (3 months) and year-to-date earnings; if self-employed, profit/loss and business bank statements
- Bank statements (last 3–6 months), retirement and investment account statements
- Mortgage/lease, utilities, major recurring bills, debt statements
- Childcare, medical, daycare, school tuition receipts and anticipated costs
- Health insurance info and out-of-pocket medical expenses for the child
- School records, IEPs, special education documents if applicable
- Pediatrician/medical records relevant to custody issues
- Communication logs with the other parent (texts, emails, calendar entries) — preserve originals and backup
- Police reports, protection orders, incident reports (if any)
- Photos, videos, or witness contact info that support custody or safety concerns
- A timeline of significant events (custody changes, incidents, moves, missed payments)
- Parenting schedules you propose (regular schedule, holiday schedule, summer schedule)

What the status conference typically looks like (what the judge will do)
- Confirm parties and counsel are present or represented
- Determine jurisdiction and service status
- Ask if temporary orders are needed or already in place
- Set a litigation schedule (deadlines for discovery, motions, mediation, custody evaluations)
- Refer parties to mediation, conciliation services, or parenting education (common in family courts)
- Possibly set a future pretrial or trial date if the case is contested
- Encourage settlement or narrow disputed issues

Typical next steps and approximate timeline (general)
- Pre-conference: gather documents, discuss strategy with your attorney (1–4 weeks)
- Status conference: judge sets schedule and next deadlines (on scheduled date)
- Discovery phase: exchange financials and evidence (1–3 months, depending on complexity)
- Mediation/conciliation: often required before trial (1–2 months to schedule)
- Custody evaluation or GAL: if ordered, can take several months and include interviews, home visits, psychological testing (2–6 months)
- Motions and hearings: interim hearings on temporary orders as needed
- Pretrial and trial: if no settlement, trial scheduling varies (4–12+ months depending on court backlog)
- Post-trial: enforcement or appeals if necessary

Practical tips and preparation
- Be organized: create a folder (physical and digital) labeled and indexed by document type and date.
- Preserve communications: don’t delete texts, emails, voicemails, or social-media messages that may be relevant.
- Avoid posting anything about the case or children on social media.
- Keep a neutral, factual communication style with the other parent; document exchanges.
- If safety is a concern, tell your attorney immediately—shelters, protection orders, or emergency relief might be necessary.
- Ask your attorney for a written timeline and a short checklist of immediate items before the status conference.

Sample things to say to your attorney at your next meeting
- “Can you walk me through what the judge is likely to set at the status conference and what you would like me to prepare?”
- “What temporary orders should we seek now, if any?”
- “Which documents from the list above would be most helpful for you to prepare pleadings/support a temporary order?”
- “What is our realistic timeline and budget to get through discovery, mediation, and toward trial if necessary?”

Would you like me to:
- Draft an email to Robert Kim summarizing these questions and asking for guidance about the status conference? (I can draft it and save as a draft or send it if you want.)
- Create a printable checklist you can bring to your meeting?
- Help organize a document list or template (financial summary, parenting-time proposal, timeline of events)?

Tell me which of those you want next, and please confirm the correct court (Hennepin County, MN or Denver) and the date of the status conference if you have it.

