I can help you get organized. I’m not a lawyer, but I can list the key things to ask your attorney, the documents to gather, and a typical timeline/next steps so you can be prepared for the status conference and the months ahead. If you’d like, I can also draft an email to Robert Kim summarizing these questions and asking for a meeting.

1) Short legal disclaimer
- I’m not providing legal advice. Use these suggestions to guide your conversation with Robert Kim at Kim & Associates.

2) Immediate priorities (what to do now)
- Schedule a meeting with Robert Kim well before the status conference (ask for at least 1–2 weeks before).
- If there are any safety concerns (domestic violence, threats, child safety), tell your attorney immediately—there may be emergency or protective orders available.
- Start collecting the documents below now (some items take time).

3) Documents to gather (high priority first)
- Child’s: birth certificate, school enrollment records, immunizations/medical records, therapists’ notes if applicable.
- Existing court orders, temporary orders, prior agreements, or protection orders.
- Communications: relevant text messages, e-mails, call logs, social media posts between you and the other parent about parenting/time/support.
- Financial documents (last 2–3 years if available):
  - Most recent pay stubs (3–6 months)
  - Last 2–3 years of tax returns and W-2s/1099s
  - Bank statements (3–12 months)
  - Retirement and investment account statements
  - Mortgage or rent statements, utility bills
  - Credit card statements and other debt info
  - Childcare/income-related expense receipts
  - Health insurance and medical bills for child/you
- Work schedule and childcare schedule (if relevant to parenting time).
- Any police reports, medical records, photos, witness names that relate to safety or parenting concerns.
- A calendar or log of parenting time issues, missed exchanges, or incidents (dates and short notes).

4) Key topics and questions to ask Robert Kim (grouped by theme)
A. Case status & next steps
- What is the purpose of the status conference in our case, and what will the judge likely ask?
- What deadlines will be set at the status conference (discovery, mediation, evaluations, motions, trial date)?
- What should I file or bring to the status conference (proposed scheduling order, temporary orders request, financial affidavits)?

B. Custody/parenting time (legal and physical custody)
- How does Minnesota define legal custody vs. physical custody / parenting time and how does that apply to my situation?
- What outcome should I consider asking for (sole custody, joint custody, specific parenting-time schedule)?
- What evidence will be important to support my position about parenting time (child’s needs, schedules, stability)?
- Would a parenting plan or proposed schedule help; can you draft one with me?

C. Child support and support issues
- How is child support calculated in Minnesota and can you estimate the guideline amount for my situation?
- What financial documentation will you need to prepare an accurate support calculation?
- Do I have grounds to request temporary child support now or any deviation from guideline support?

D. Temporary orders and emergency relief
- Should we seek temporary orders now for custody, parenting time, child support, spousal maintenance, or exclusive use of the home?
- How long do temporary orders last and how are they enforced?

E. Discovery and evidence
- What discovery will we request (financial disclosures, custody evaluations, school or medical records)?
- Should we request a custody evaluation, guardian ad litem, or mental health evaluation for the child?
- What witnesses or documentation should I identify now?

F. Settlement, mediation, and litigation strategy
- Will this case likely go to mediation or settlement conference? Does Hennepin County require or encourage mediation/Family Court services?
- What are the pros/cons of settlement vs. going to trial in our case?
- What is our likely timeline and estimated costs if we go to trial?

G. Practical steps & communications
- How should I communicate with the other parent (text only, co-parenting apps)? What should I avoid?
- What immediate changes should I make to my finances or living arrangements (if any)?
- Will you seek or object to exchange arrangements, supervised visitation, or restrictions?

H. Costs and logistics
- What are your fees, likely costs (e.g., experts, evaluations), and billing practices going forward?
- How often will we meet or update the case, and how quickly will you respond to urgent matters?

I. Rights and protections (what you should understand)
- My rights regarding parenting time, legal custody decisions, and child support under Minnesota law.
- My right to request temporary orders and to ask for the court to order evaluations, discovery, or evidence production.
