First — quick clarification: you mentioned both “family court in Denver” and “Hennepin County, Minnesota.” Which is the actual court handling the case? The answers below assume Hennepin County / Minnesota (you said Hennepin later), but I can tailor anything to Denver if that’s the correct jurisdiction.

Below is a focused, practical checklist to help you organize your thoughts and prepare for your status conference and for your meeting with your attorney (Robert Kim). You can take this into the meeting or I can turn it into an email or printable checklist for you.

1) Immediate clarifying questions to ask Robert Kim
- Jurisdiction and timeline
  - Which court has jurisdiction (confirm Hennepin County vs Denver)?
  - What will happen at the status conference the judge set in two months?
  - Are there any immediate deadlines I need to know about (financial disclosures, motions)?
- Custody / parenting time rights
  - What types of custody/orders are available in my case (legal custody, physical custody, joint vs sole)?
  - What rights do I have regarding parenting time, decision-making for schooling, health care, and extracurriculars?
  - If I’m worried about relocation or travel with the child, what protections can we seek now?
- Child support / spousal support
  - How will child support be calculated here (what income is included)?
  - What documents do you need to calculate support (pay stubs, tax returns, child care expenses, health insurance)?
  - Are temporary child support and/or spousal support orders likely or necessary before final orders?
- Temporary/emergency orders and safety
  - Should we seek temporary custody/support or a restraining order now (if there are safety concerns)?
  - What is the fastest way to get emergency relief if needed?
- Discovery, evidence, and experts
  - What discovery will we request (financial records, communications, school records)?
  - Do you recommend a custody evaluation, guardian ad litem (GAL), parenting coordinator, or forensic accountant?
  - What evidence is most important to build my case?
- Settlement / mediation / trial strategy
  - Will the court require mediation or parenting education in our county? If so, how soon?
  - What is your recommended strategy — negotiation/mediation or preparing for trial?
  - What are realistic likely outcomes for custody and support in my situation?
- Fees, costs, and logistics
  - What is your estimate of fees to get through the next stages (status conference, discovery, possible trial)?
  - How will communications work (email, phone, client portal) and typical response time?
- Enforcement & modifications
  - If the other parent violates an order, what are my enforcement options?
  - How easy/hard is it to request a modification later if circumstances change?

2) Documents to gather now (bring these to your meeting and to the status conference if requested)
- Identification and relationship: child’s birth certificate, proof of parentage, any existing court orders.
- Financial documents (last 2–3 years if available): pay stubs, W-2s/1099s, last 2 years tax returns, bank statements, retirement statements, mortgage/lease, credit card statements, debt details.
- Child-related expenses: daycare, medical bills, receipts for extracurriculars, school tuition, and health insurance information.
- Communications: relevant texts, emails, social media messages between you and the other parent (organized chronologically).
- Parenting logs: calendars showing who had the child when, any missed visitation, notable incidents.
- School and medical: report cards, attendance records, IEPs or special needs documentation, medical reports.
- Witness list and contact information: family members, teachers, coaches, neighbors who can speak to parenting or the child’s needs.
- Any police reports, protective orders, or records of domestic incidents.

3) What to prepare for the status conference (two months out)
- Short written summary of the issues you want the court to address (custody, support, temporary orders).
- Proposed timeline and proposed deadlines you want the judge to set (discovery cut-off, mediation, expert deadlines, trial date).
- A proposed interim parenting plan or schedule if you want temporary parenting time orders.
- A short list of outstanding discovery you will request and any emergency relief you seek.
- Ask your lawyer what the judge typically asks at status conferences in your county.

4) Typical next steps in custody/support/divorce cases (general flow — timeline varies by case & county)
- Status conference: judge sets scheduling order, deadlines, and next appearance.
- Temporary orders hearing (if needed): for parenting time/support while case proceeds.
- Mandatory mediation or settlement conference (often required before trial).
- Discovery: written requests, interrogatories, document production, depositions (financial disclosures are common).
- Evaluations/experts (if ordered or recommended): custody evaluator, mental health evaluator, financial forensic review.
- Pretrial motions & hearings: evidentiary issues, motions to compel discovery, emergency motions if circumstances change.
- Trial: if no settlement — judge (or jury in very rare cases) issues final orders.
- Post-judgment: enforcement or modification as circumstances change.

5) Minnesota-specific notes (if this is indeed Hennepin County)
- Child support follows state guidelines — incomes and parenting time affect the calculation.
- Courts consider the “best interests of the child” factors when deciding custody/parenting time (stability, parent-child relationship, child’s needs and preference if appropriate, etc.).
- Many counties require or encourage mediation and parenting education; there may be local rules or required forms — your attorney will know Hennepin’s procedures.
- If custody evaluations are recommended, expect them to take several weeks to months.

6) Evidence and credibility — what matters most
- Clear, contemporaneous records (calendars, logs, texts) are more persuasive than generalized claims.
- Financial documentation is essential to support or dispute support requests.
- Balanced focus on the child’s best interests (stability, routine, schooling, health) rather than personal grievances.

7) Practical safety & communication tips before you meet
- If safety is an issue, tell your lawyer immediately and avoid direct contact with the other parent — preserve messages and document incidents.
- Avoid posting about the case or the child on social media.
- Keep communications focused and, when possible, go through counsel.
- Start a simple parenting log/calendar (dates/times, who had the child, missed pickups, noteworthy events).

8) Questions to prioritize in the meeting (short list you can read aloud)
- What will happen at the status conference and what do you want from me before then?
- Do we need temporary orders now — for custody, parenting time, or support?
- Which documents should I get to you this week?
- Do you expect mediation, and how should I prepare?
- What is the estimated timeline and cost to get to a final resolution?
- If I’m offered a settlement, what are the main tradeoffs I should consider?

9) Offer to help
- I can draft a short email to Robert Kim summarizing the situation and requesting a meeting or listing these questions — would you like that?
- I can also format these as a one-page printable checklist or a fillable prep worksheet for your meeting.

Next step from me: tell me whether the court is Hennepin County (Minnesota) or Denver (Colorado), whether you want me to draft an email to Robert Kim, and whether there are any urgent safety or emergency issues. If you’d like the email, tell me the tone (formal or concise) and any specific facts you want included (e.g., date of status conference, any existing orders).

