I can help — this is a lot to organize, and being prepared will make the status conference and the next steps much smoother. A couple quick clarifying points before I get into the checklist and suggested questions:

- You mentioned family court in Denver and also Hennepin County, Minnesota. Which court is this actually in? That changes local procedures and rules.  
- I’m not giving legal advice; the items below are general guidance to help you prepare questions and documents to review with your attorney (Robert Kim at Kim & Associates).

Suggested questions to ask your attorney (prioritize these at your meeting or in an email)
- Case basics and timeline
  - Can you confirm the court, case number, and the status conference date/time/location?
  - What is the judge likely to address at the status conference?
  - What deadlines or events should I expect next (discovery deadlines, mediation, evaluation, trial date)?
- Custody / parenting time
  - What are my legal rights regarding custody and parenting time under the applicable state law (what standard does the judge use)?
  - How does the court evaluate “best interests” in this jurisdiction? What factors matter most here?
  - Should we propose a temporary parenting-time schedule before the status conference? If so, what should it include?
  - What steps should we take if the other parent is not cooperating with agreed parenting time or is withholding the child?
  - How might relocation or proposed changes to the child’s school/primary residence be handled?
- Child support and financials
  - How will child support be calculated in this state and what documents do you need to calculate it?
  - Are there interim (temporary) support orders we should request now?
  - If there are combined custody/time-sharing arrangements, how does that affect support?
  - What financial disclosures will be required (pay stubs, tax returns, bank statements, retirement accounts, debts)?
- Temporary/emergency orders
  - Should we seek temporary orders for custody, child support, spousal support, or exclusive use of the home?
  - What constitutes an emergency that would justify an emergency order here?
- Evidence, discovery, and investigations
  - What evidence will be most important for our custody/support arguments (texts, emails, school records, medical records, photos, witnesses)?
  - What discovery should we pursue (interrogatories, document requests, depositions)?
  - Would you recommend any evaluations — custody evaluator, parental fitness, substance abuse testing, mental health evaluation, forensic accountant?
- Alternative dispute resolution & settlement
  - Do you recommend mediation or parenting coordination? Is mediation required in this county?
  - What settlement positions should I prepare and what are realistic outcomes?
- Costs, communication, and strategy
  - What is your recommended short-term strategy leading up to the status conference and the next 3–6 months?
  - How will we communicate (email, phone, client portal)? What response times can I expect?
  - What are estimated legal fees and likely additional costs (mediator, evaluator, expert witness)?
- Next steps & paperwork
  - What should I file (or sign) before the status conference? Do you need any documents from me now?
  - What should I bring to the status conference?
- If there are safety/abuse concerns
  - How will allegations of abuse or safety concerns be handled? Should we seek protective orders?
  - How do we document and present evidence of abuse or neglect to the court?

Documents and information to gather (start collecting these now)
- Basic case and identification: court papers, case number, contact info for opposing party and counsel
- Financial documents (past 2–3 years if possible): tax returns, W-2s/1099s, pay stubs, bank statements, retirement statements, mortgage/lease, bills, credit card statements
- Child-specific documents: birth certificate, school records, attendance/excuse notes, IEPs, medical and dental records, therapy records
- Communication logs: emails, text messages, call logs showing parenting-time issues, a calendar of missed visits or incidents
- Witness names and brief summaries (teachers, doctors, relatives, neighbors)
- Any police reports, protection orders, or court documents involving either parent
- Records of childcare expenses, extracurriculars, and special needs expenses
- Your proposed parenting-time schedule and a brief narrative of your desired outcome

What to expect at the status conference
- Typically the judge or magistrate will:
  - Confirm the case issues (custody, support, property if applicable)
  - Set a schedule for discovery and deadlines (financial disclosures, expert reports)
  - Order or recommend mediation and/or parenting classes
  - Decide whether a custody evaluation or other assessments are needed
  - Set trial date or a deadline to propose settlement
  - Consider any temporary orders (often this is done by agreement or separate motion)
- Bring proposed orders or a short written statement of what you want if the attorney advises doing so.

Immediate actions you can take in the next 2 months
- Confirm the correct court and date; tell your attorney if there’s any conflict.
- Start collecting the documents listed above and organize them (digital folders help).
- Keep a contemporaneous parenting log for dates/times of exchanges, missed pickups, concerning incidents.
- Draft a proposed parenting time schedule and a short “position statement” (1–2 pages) summarizing your concerns and goals.
- Discuss with your attorney whether to file any temporary-motion (support, custody, exclusive residence).
- Ask about and schedule mediation or evaluations early if recommended — these can take time.
- If safety is a concern, ask about emergency orders now — don’t wait.

How I can help right now
- I can draft an email or checklist to Robert Kim that summarizes these questions and asks for next steps (I can tailor the tone: concise/urgent or conversational).  
- I can create a printable checklist of documents and a timeline to bring to your appointment.  
- If you want, I can draft a short “position statement” you can review with your lawyer.

Would you like me to:
1) Draft an email to Robert Kim with the prioritized questions and a request to confirm the court and status-conference expectations?  
2) Create a one-page checklist (documents and tasks) you can use in the next 2 months?  

Also please confirm which court (Denver or Hennepin County, MN) so I can tailor the guidance to local procedures.

