I can help you get organized. I’m not a lawyer, but I can outline useful questions, a checklist of documents to gather, and typical next steps so you can be prepared for your status conference and your meeting with Robert Kim. Before I go into details, one quick clarification:

- You said proceedings are at family court in Denver and also in Hennepin County, Minnesota. Which court will hold the upcoming status conference (Denver or Hennepin)? If both jurisdictions are involved, that’s important to know.

If you want, I can draft an email to Robert Kim with these questions and the items you’ll bring — tell me and I’ll prepare it. Otherwise, here’s a structured plan you can use.

1) High‑priority clarifying questions to ask your attorney (Robert Kim)
- Which jurisdiction’s court has authority over custody/support issues here (Denver or Hennepin)? If both, how do we handle jurisdictional conflict?
- What are my immediate rights regarding custody/parenting time and child support while the case is pending?
- What should I expect at the status conference in two months? (Purpose, typical orders issued, what I should bring)
- Should we seek temporary orders now (custody, parenting time, child support, child expenses, health insurance)?
- Do we need a guardian ad litem, custody evaluator, social study, or other expert? If so, when is that requested/ordered and who pays?
- What is the court’s local practice for mediation or alternative dispute resolution in family cases?
- What documents and financial disclosures will the court require (affidavit of income, tax returns, pay stubs, account statements)?
- What is the likely timeline to resolution (settlement or trial) and the stages we should expect?
- What discovery will we need (interrogatories, document requests, depositions)? How long does discovery usually take here?
- Are there steps I should take now to protect my parental rights (e.g., preserve evidence, file motions, request emergency relief)?
- How will custody be decided here (standard used, factors the judge considers)?
- How is child support calculated in this jurisdiction and what expenses beyond base support should I expect to address (insurance, childcare, education, extracurriculars)?
- What are estimated attorney fees, filing fees, and other likely costs? Can we seek fee shifting from the other party?
- If the other parent is hostile or there are safety concerns, what immediate protections can we request (restraining order, supervised exchange, emergency hearing)?
- How will communication with you be handled (email, phone, billing, frequency)? Who is my point of contact at the firm?

2) Documents and records to gather now
- Child’s birth certificate(s), Social Security number (if available), school enrollment records, immunization/medical records
- Any existing court orders or agreements (temporary orders, parenting plans, prior custody/support orders)
- Calendar/timeline of important events (move-outs, incidents, schooling, major disputes)
- Records of communication with the other parent: text messages, emails, social media messages, call logs (sorted by date and preserved)
- Police reports, medical records, photos, witness contact info if there are safety or abuse concerns
- Financial documents (last 2–3 years of tax returns, last 3–6 months of pay stubs, W-2s, 1099s, bank statements, retirement account statements, investment statements, mortgage/lease, credit card and loan statements)
- Proof of monthly household expenses and child-related expenses (childcare, medical premiums, uninsured medical costs, school fees)
- Employment info and schedules (work hours, flexibility, travel)
- Records of financial support already exchanged (cash, bank transfers), receipts for child expenses
- Any other documents showing the child’s living situation or routines (school attendance, extracurriculars)

3) Typical next steps and timeline (what usually happens after a status conference)
- Status conference: court issues a scheduling order (deadlines for discovery, motions, mediation, and trial date window)
- Temporary orders (if needed): either by agreement or hearing; these can set interim custody, parenting time, and support
- Disclosure and discovery: exchange of financial documents, written questions (interrogatories), requests for documents, sometimes depositions
- Mediation/ADR: many family courts require mediation before trial on custody/support
- Evaluations (if ordered): custody evaluation, mental health evaluation, home study — can take weeks to months
- Motions: either side can bring motions (for emergency relief, to compel discovery, for sanctions)
- Settlement negotiation: majority of family matters resolve by agreement before trial
- Trial: if no settlement, a custody/support trial where judge issues final orders
- Post-judgment motions and enforcement: if orders aren’t followed, motions to enforce or modify later

4) Specific custody/support issues to discuss in detail
- Legal vs. physical custody: what decisions you can make for daily care vs. major decisions (education, medical, religion)
- Parenting time schedule: weekdays, weekends, holiday schedule, summer schedule, exchanges, pickup/drop-off logistics
- Relocation: what restrictions apply if either parent wants to move with the child; notice requirements
- Child support: base calculation, healthcare cost allocation, daycare/childcare, extraordinary expenses (education, therapy), tax exemptions
- Insurance and benefits: who provides health insurance; responsibility for premiums and uncovered costs
- Enforcement tools: wage withholding/garnishment, contempt, income withholding
- Modification standard: when and how custody or support can be modified later

5) If safety or abuse is an issue
- Tell your lawyer immediately. Ask about emergency orders, supervised visitation, safe exchange arrangements, and how to document threats or incidents.
- Preserve all evidence: screenshots, recordings (check state law re: recording), police reports, medical records.

6) Prepare your goals and strategy before meeting
- Write down your top priorities (e.g., primary custody, defined parenting time, joint decision-making, adequate support).
- Identify three negotiable items and three non-negotiables so your attorney can evaluate settlement flexibility.
- Prepare a short factual timeline (one page) of the relationship and recent events relevant to custody/support.

7) What to bring to your meeting with Robert Kim (or to the status conference if requested)
- Copies of the documents above (financials, communications, court filings)
- A concise one-page summary of the case and your goals
- List of witnesses or people who can attest to parenting or incidents
- Any current court notices or hearing dates

8) Sample agenda for the status conference (what to ask the judge/court through your attorney)
- Request scheduling deadlines for discovery, mediation, motions, and a trial window
- Ask whether temporary orders will be set and the process/timeline for obtaining them
- Confirm any local parenting education or mediation requirements
- Confirm whether the court expects custody evaluations and timing/fees

9) Communication and next steps I can help with
- I can draft a clear email to Robert Kim listing these questions and attaching a one-page timeline and checklist. Tell me if you want that and what tone (brief, detailed).
- I can make a printable checklist of documents to collect.
- I can help draft a short timeline of events you can bring to meetings or court.
- If you want, tell me any specific facts you’re comfortable sharing (e.g., existing temporary orders, safety issues, income concerns) and I’ll tailor the questions and checklist.

Next step from me: confirm which court the upcoming status conference is in (Denver or Hennepin), and tell me if there are immediate safety concerns or existing temporary orders. Want me to draft an email to Robert Kim with these questions and attach your one-page summary?

