Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
First, one quick clarification: you said proceedings at family court in Denver, then said Hennepin County, Minnesota. Which is it? Jurisdiction affects procedure and which local rules apply. Tell me which county/state so I can tailor the checklist more precisely. Meanwhile, here’s a practical, jurisdiction‑neutral plan you can use now to get organized and make the most of the status conference and your meetings with Robert Kim.

What the status conference usually is for (what to expect)
- The judge will typically: confirm the parties and counsel are ready, set a schedule for the case (discovery deadlines, motion deadlines, mediation/parenting evaluation, pretrial), address temporary orders if needed, and decide whether a settlement process (mediation, custody evaluation) is required.
- It’s usually short — the judge sets deadlines and a next court date. Be prepared to report on settlement discussions and any immediate needs (temporary custody/support).

Immediate actions (next 1–2 weeks)
- Confirm jurisdiction (Denver vs Hennepin) with your attorney.
- Schedule a meeting with Robert Kim to go over strategy before the status conference.
- Collect and organize documents (see checklist below).
- Start a clear calendar/timeline of events and communications relevant to custody/support.
- Decide (with your attorney) whether you’ll request any temporary orders now (temporary custody, parenting time, support, or exclusive use of home).

Documents to gather and organize
- Court filings and pleadings (petition, response, temporary orders, prior judgments).
- Any communication with the other parent (texts, emails, social media) relevant to parenting or disputes.
- Parenting history: proposed schedule, school/daycare records, immunizations, medical and counseling records for children.
- Income and financial documents for you and the other party (recent pay stubs, last 2 years of tax returns, W-2s/1099s, bank statements, retirement account statements, mortgage/lease, credit card statements, debt info).
- Child-related expenses: childcare, medical, school costs, extracurriculars, receipts.
- Evidence of special circumstances: substance abuse, domestic violence, mental health issues, police reports, restraining orders, supervision needs.
- Work schedules and availability for parenting time.
- A succinct timeline of important events (moves, incidents, changes in parenting, major expenses).

Questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic)
Custody / Parenting time
- What custody terms are likely (legal custody, physical custody, primary residence vs shared) given my situation?
- How does the court in our jurisdiction evaluate "best interests" of the child? Which factors matter most here?
- What parenting plan should I propose? Can you draft one for the court?
- If the other parent wants relocation or extended travel, how is that handled?
- What evidence will be most persuasive (witnesses, records, school/medical testimony)?
- If there’s a history/concern of domestic violence, how do we ask for supervised visitation or protections?

Child support & financial
- How is child support calculated here? Can you provide an estimate/explain the guideline worksheet?
- Should we request temporary child support right away? How soon are temporary orders effective?
- How are childcare, medical expenses, and extracurriculars handled by the court?
- Are spousal support/alimony issues likely here? If so, how are temporary spousal support and asset division handled?

Case process & next steps
- What will likely happen at the status conference and who should attend?
- What schedule will the judge likely set (discovery, motions deadline, mediation, trial date)?
- Should we pursue mediation or a custody evaluation (and what do those cost and how long do they take)?
- What discovery will we need? Will we serve interrogatories, document requests, or seek depositions?
- If we need expert witnesses (e.g., child psychologist), when should we retain them?

Strategy & settlement
- What are our realistic goals and what’s a reasonable settlement range?
- What is your recommended negotiation strategy with opposing counsel?
- Which issues are most important to litigate vs settle?
- How should I respond to direct outreach from the other parent?

Practical & administrative
- What should I bring to the status conference and to our next meeting?
- How will you keep me updated (phone, email, client portal) and how often?
- What are estimated legal fees, likely costs (experts, mediation), and billing practices? Can we get a cost estimate to trial?
- If an emergency arises (safety, child removal), what should I do and who do I call?

What you should do to prepare before the status conference
- Meet briefly with Robert Kim to prioritize objectives and discuss what you want from the case (custody arrangement, support, timeline).
- Provide your attorney with the documents above and a one‑page summary of key facts and the timeline.
- Prepare a short statement of what you want the judge to know in the status conference (if asked).
- Decide whether you want to ask the court for temporary orders (custody, support, exclusive use of the home).

Possible requests to make at the status conference
- A clear scheduling order with discovery deadlines and a trial date or stay if settlement talks are active.
- Referral to mediation or parenting education classes.
- Temporary custody/visitation and temporary child support.
- Request for a custody evaluation or parenting assessment if appropriate.

How discovery and evidence typically progress
- Early exchanges: Mandatory financial disclosures and documents.
- Discovery tools: document requests, interrogatories, subpoenas for records (school, medical), depositions in some cases.
- Experts: custody evaluators or child psychologists if a factual dispute about parenting capacity exists.
- Be thorough: organize exhibits and timelines early — judges appreciate succinct, well-documented presentations.

Safety and protection issues
- If you have any safety concerns (threats, violence, intimidation, child endangerment), tell your attorney immediately. Courts take those issues seriously and there are emergency remedies.
- Preserve evidence: screenshots, police reports, medical records, witness contact info.

Checklist to bring to your meeting with Robert Kim
- One‑page goals/priorities (what outcome you want)
- Timeline of events and important dates
- Copies of filings and pleadings
- Financial documents (taxes, paystubs, recent bank statements)
- Child-related records (school/medical)
- Communication logs (texts, emails)
- Any police/restraining order documents

Notes on costs and timeframes
- Many cases settle before trial, often after mediation or a custody evaluation.
- Expect several months from status conference to resolution in typical contested cases, longer if trial is necessary.
- Costs vary widely depending on whether you use experts or go to trial—ask Robert for an estimate and possible cost‑saving alternatives.

Practical communication tips with the other parent
- Keep records of all communications; use written channels when possible.
- Avoid inflammatory language; court looks unfavorably on hostility.
- Document missed visitations, canceled appointments, or incidents affecting the child.

Next step I can help with right now
- If you’d like, I can draft a concise email or agenda you can send to Robert Kim summarizing these questions and requesting a pre‑status conference meeting. Tell me if you want it formal or conversational and whether to include the jurisdiction clarification (Denver vs Hennepin), and I’ll draft that for you.

Finally: I’m not a lawyer. This is general information to help you prepare—confirm all strategy and legal questions with Robert Kim. Tell me which county is the correct jurisdiction and whether you want that draft email to your attorney.

