- Financial records for both parents if available: recent pay stubs, W-2/1099 and last 2–3 years tax returns, bank statements, credit card statements, mortgage/lease, retirement accounts, debts
- Monthly expense list for child (daycare, extracurriculars, medical, insurance)
- Work schedules, proof of primary caregiving (calendars, photos, witnesses)
- Evidence of parenting involvement (sports, school emails, receipts)
- Any police reports, protection orders, medical records related to safety concerns
- Records of child support or spousal support paid/received previously
- Contact list of witnesses who can testify about parenting or safety concerns

Typical timeline / next steps in a family law custody/support case
- Initial meeting with attorney and fact-gathering (you’re here)
- Filing or responding to pleadings (if not already filed)
- Service of process and initial disclosures
- Temporary orders or emergency motions (custody, support, exclusive possession)
- Discovery (interrogatories, document requests, deposition practice)
- Alternative dispute resolution: mediation or settlement conferences (often required or strongly encouraged)
- Status conferences (the judge reviews scheduling — your next one in two months)
- Possible custody evaluations or guardian ad litem investigation (if ordered)
- Pretrial motions and hearings
- Trial, if no settlement
- Final order and then possible post-judgment motions (modification, enforcement, contempt)

What to expect at the status conference
- The judge will want a case schedule: deadlines for discovery, mediation, evaluator appointments, and the likely trial window
- The judge may ask whether parties have explored settlement/mediation, whether either party needs temporary orders, and what unresolved issues exist
- Prepare a short summary for your attorney of the contested issues and any urgent needs (safety, school, relocation)
- Bring proposed timelines and any immediate requests you want the judge to set

Strategic considerations to review with your attorney
- Your top priorities for custody and parenting time, and which issues you can compromise on
- Whether to seek joint legal custody vs. sole legal custody, and why
- Whether to pursue mediation early (often less expensive) or prepare for a contested process
- Whether to request parenting evaluations or a guardian ad litem (useful in high conflict but costly)
- How to present financials to best reflect child support needs
- Protecting children’s stability — schooling, healthcare, travel, and routines

Safety and sensitive issues
- If there’s any risk to you or the children (abuse, threats), tell your attorney immediately — emergency orders and safety planning can be requested
- Keep records of any incidents and avoid direct confrontations; preserve evidence

