Thanks — I can help you get organized. A couple quick clarifying questions before I make a detailed checklist and suggested questions for your attorney:
- Which court is handling this matter? You mentioned both Denver family court and Hennepin County, Minnesota. Which one is the active case?
- Do you already have a status conference date and any temporary orders in place (custody, support, no-contact, etc.)?

While you answer, here’s a focused, practical set of items: what to ask your attorney about your rights, a timeline of typical next steps, and a document checklist you can bring to your meeting.

What to ask your attorney (high-priority)
- Jurisdiction & status
  - Which court actually has jurisdiction (confirm Denver vs. Hennepin County) and any local rules we need to follow?
  - Has the opposing party been properly served? Are all filings up-to-date?
- Immediate protections and temporary orders
  - Are there existing temporary orders for custody, parenting time, or support? Should we ask the court for temporary orders now?
  - If I’m worried about safety or urgent issues, what emergency relief is available and how quickly can we get it?
- Custody specifics and your rights
  - What types of custody orders are possible here (legal decision-making, physical custody, joint vs. sole) under this court’s standards?
  - What factors will the court consider in a custody determination in this jurisdiction?
  - How do relocation requests work if the other parent wants to move with the child?
- Child support and finances
  - How will temporary and final child support be calculated (what guideline is used)?
  - What financial documentation will the court require? Do we need to prepare a budget or affidavit of income/expenses?
- Process, timeline & strategy
  - What usually happens at a status conference in this court? What will the judge likely decide or order?
  - What is your recommended litigation/settlement strategy and likely timeline to resolution or trial?
  - What are the important deadlines we should expect (discovery, evaluations, mediation, pretrial)?
- Discovery and evidence
  - What evidence do we need to support custody and support positions (emails, texts, school records, medical, police reports)?
  - Should we ask for a custody evaluation, and when? What experts might we need (psychologist, vocational/financial)?
  - What discovery should we issue (interrogatories, document requests, depositions)?
- Mediation and settlement
  - Is mediation required in this county? When does it usually happen?
  - What are the pros and cons of settlement vs. trial here given my facts?
- Costs and logistics
  - What is the estimated cost to proceed to trial vs. reach settlement? How are expert fees handled?
  - How often should we meet and what should I prepare for each meeting?
- Communications & next steps
  - How will we communicate (email, calls)? What’s your response time?
  - What should I do or avoid saying to the other parent or third parties right now?
- Contempt, enforcement & modification
  - How are orders enforced and what remedies exist for violations?
  - How easily can orders be modified later if circumstances change?
- Confidentiality and record-keeping
  - What should I keep a record of (communication logs, missed parenting time, expenses)?
  - Are any filings or communications confidential or protected?

Typical next steps and timeline (what usually happens)
- Immediately (days–weeks)
  - Confirm jurisdiction and service.
  - Gather documents (see checklist below) and exchange initial financial disclosures if required.
  - File or respond to any emergency or temporary order motions if needed.
- Before the status conference (weeks–months)
  - File any motions needed for temporary relief.
  - Attend initial meetings with attorney to set priorities and strategy.
  - Provide the attorney all relevant documents and a written chronology of events.
- Status conference (as scheduled)
  - Judge typically reviews case status, may enter temporary orders, set a discovery schedule, and set deadlines (mediation, evaluations, trial date or next hearing).
  - The court may refer the case to mediation, custody evaluation, or other services.
- Post-status conference
  - Complete discovery (exchange documents, take depositions).
