Thanks — I can help you get organized for the next steps. First, quick clarification so advice is targeted:

- Which court and state is handling this matter: the Family Court in Denver (Colorado) or Hennepin County Family Court (Minnesota)? Jurisdiction and procedures differ, and that will change specific forms, timelines, and local rules.
- Are you currently represented by Robert Kim at Kim & Associates (and do you want questions to send to him), or are you meeting/retaining him soon?

Once you confirm those, I can tailor wording and local next-step items. Meanwhile, here’s a practical, ready-to-use set of questions and steps you can use at your meeting or call with your attorney, plus a checklist of documents to gather.

Questions to ask your attorney (priority order)
- Jurisdiction and venue
  - Which state/court’s laws apply? Is Hennepin County or Denver the correct venue, and why?
  - Are there any immediate jurisdictional issues or deadlines I should know about?

- Case status & immediate next steps
  - What will happen at the status conference in two months? What should I be prepared to present?
  - What deadlines will be set at the status conference?
  - Should we file any temporary orders (custody, parenting time, child support, spousal support, exclusive use of the home) before the status conference?

- Custody/parenting-time issues
  - What types of custody (legal and physical) arrangements are likely under these circumstances?
  - What evidence or documentation strengthens my case for custody/parenting time?
  - Will the court order a custody evaluation, home study, or parental responsibilities evaluation? If so, what is the process and timeline?

- Child support & financial support
  - How is child support calculated in this jurisdiction? What numbers/documents are needed?
  - Should we seek temporary support orders right away? How are temporary support amounts determined?
  - Are there likely to be spousal support/alimony issues? If so, how will they be determined?

- Discovery & evidence
  - What financial disclosures are required (pay stubs, tax returns, bank statements, retirement, debts, business records)?
  - Do we need to subpoena records, obtain phone/location data, or preserve social media posts?
  - Who should be listed as witnesses, and how do we preserve or document their testimony?

- Settlement vs trial strategy
  - What are our settlement options (mediation, negotiation, collaborative law)? Do you recommend mediation?
  - What is the realistic timeline and cost if we go to trial?
  - What are the strengths and weaknesses of my position?

- Safety & urgent issues
  - If there are safety concerns or domestic violence, how do we get emergency orders or protect the children?
  - What should I do if the other parent violates custody or support orders?

- Communication & logistics with your attorney
  - Who is my primary contact at the firm? How should I communicate (email, phone, portal)?
  - What are your billing rates, retainer, and estimated costs through the status conference and trial?
  - How often will we meet or receive updates?

Documents to gather (bring to your meeting)
- Child: birth certificates, school enrollment and records, immunizations, medical records, therapy records
- Parenting: calendars showing parenting time, communications about scheduling (texts, emails, app messages)
- Financial: recent pay stubs (3–6 months), last 2–3 years of tax returns, W-2s/1099s, bank statements (6–12 months), credit card statements, mortgage/lease documents, car loans, retirement account statements, business profit/loss statements (if self-employed)
- Household: deed/lease, utility bills, major asset inventories (vehicles, high-value personal property)
- Legal: any prior court orders, police reports, protection orders, prior custody agreements
- Evidence: screenshots of communications, photos, recordings if legally obtained (check local law), witness names and brief statements

What to prepare for the status conference (common expectations)
- A summary of current custody/visitation arrangements and any disputes
- A short list of immediate requests (temporary custody, child support, parenting time)
- Proposed timeline for discovery and mediation
- Confirmation of whether there are safety issues or urgent relief needed
- Be ready to propose or accept a scheduling order (deadlines for discovery, evaluations, mediation, trial date)

Typical next-step timeline (general)
- Status conference: court sets schedule and deadlines
- Temporary orders hearing (if needed): addresses interim custody/support
- Discovery phase: exchange of financial and other documents; depositions if necessary
- Alternative dispute resolution: mediation or parenting coordination (often required or recommended)
- Custody evaluation or guardian ad litem (in some cases)
- Settlement negotiations (ongoing)
- Trial (if no settlement): final evidence, witnesses, judicial decision
Timing and order vary by jurisdiction and complexity.

Practical tips
- Keep a dated log of parenting time exchanges, missed exchanges, and notable incidents.
- Preserve electronic evidence (export texts/emails) and avoid deleting messages.
- Avoid posting about the case on social media.
- Use a co-parenting app or keep communications in written form when possible.
- Consider asking the attorney about a parenting plan template and standard local forms.

Sample agenda you can send to your attorney before the meeting (I can draft this into an email for you)
- Confirm which court has jurisdiction (Denver vs Hennepin County)
- Explain current parenting time arrangement and immediate concerns
- Ask about the status conference goals and what you should prepare
- Discuss temporary orders (custody, support, exclusive use of home)
- Review required financial disclosures and documents to produce
- Discuss need for custody evaluation or mediation, and timing
- Confirm billing/retainer and communication preferences

Would you like me to:
- Draft a short email to Robert Kim (or whichever attorney) asking these items and requesting a meeting agenda? If yes, tell me: attorney email (or say “use on-file”), and whether you want the tone formal or conversational.
- Or would you prefer a one-page printed checklist/agenda to bring to the meeting? I can create that next.

Finally, please confirm the correct court/location (Denver or Hennepin County) so I can tailor any local procedural points or forms.

