Thanks — I can help you get organized. First: a quick clarification — you mentioned proceedings at family court in Denver and also Hennepin County, Minnesota. Which court is handling this case (Denver or Hennepin County)? That will help me tailor some procedural specifics. For now I’ll give a general, practical checklist and question list you can use with Robert Kim and at the status conference.

What to ask your attorney (grouped and prioritized)
- Case status & immediate next steps
  - What is the purpose of the status conference and what should I expect the judge to decide then?
  - What deadlines will the court likely set at the status conference (discovery schedule, mediation, temporary hearings, etc.)?
  - Do we need to request any temporary orders (parenting time, custody, support, exclusive use of the home) before the status conference or at the conference?
- Custody / parenting time
  - What custody arrangement do I currently have and what are my legal rights under our state’s law?
  - What factors will the court consider when deciding custody and parenting time?
  - Should we propose a parenting plan now or wait? If we propose one, what elements should it include?
  - If domestic violence, safety, or substance abuse is a concern: what immediate protections can we seek?
- Child support & spousal support (if applicable)
  - How is child support calculated here? What financial documents will we need?
  - Is temporary child support or spousal support likely to be ordered pending resolution?
  - How do we handle healthcare, childcare costs, and extraordinary expenses?
- Evidence, discovery, and documentation
  - What specific documents should I gather now (see list below)?
  - Do we need to serve formal discovery requests? Will we ask for records (bank, phone, social media, school, medical)?
  - Should we preserve messages, call logs, GPS/location data, and financial records?
- Settlement, mediation & cost strategy
  - Is mediation required or recommended in this court? When would it occur?
  - What are our realistic settlement options and the pros/cons of settlement vs. trial?
  - What is the expected cost and timeline if we proceed to trial?
- Experts, evaluations & investigations
  - Will a custody evaluation or parenting assessment be appropriate or likely?
  - Do we need experts (child psychologist, forensic accountant, vocational expert)? How are they paid?
- Temporary/emergency relief
  - If I need an emergency order (e.g., remove the other parent from the home, supervised visitation, or emergency support), what’s the process and timeline?
- Communication & case management
  - How will you update me (email, phone, portal) and how quickly can I expect replies?
  - Who on your team will handle day-to-day communications?
  - What should I say/avoid saying to the other parent and third parties?
- Outcomes & appeals
  - What are realistic best-case and worst-case outcomes for custody and support?
  - If I’m unhappy with a ruling, what are my options (motions, appeals) and time limits?

Documents to gather now
- Identification: birth certificates for children, driver’s license, marriage certificate
- Financial: pay stubs (last 3 months), year-to-date income, last 2 years tax returns, W-2s, 1099s, bank statements (last 12–24 months), retirement account statements, mortgage/lease, credit card statements, debts
- Child-related: daycare/school records, medical and mental-health records, vaccination records, IEPs or special education records
- Parenting records: calendars showing parenting time, travel, cancelled visits; school and extracurricular schedules
- Communications: texts, emails, social media messages, voicemail transcripts showing interactions about children or finances
- Incident records: police reports, medical records, protective orders, photographs showing injuries or home conditions
- Other: rental/utility bills, proof of household expenses, proof of extraordinary child expenses (therapy, medical)

What typically happens at a status conference (general)
- Court confirms service and jurisdiction and identifies unresolved issues
- Judge may set deadlines: exchange of financial affidavits, discovery schedule, mediation/negotiation deadlines, dates for temporary hearings, custody evaluations, and a trial-setting date
- The judge may issue or schedule temporary orders if requested
- The judge will usually expect the parties to have discussed settlement/mediation before the next conference

Typical timeline and milestones (general)
- Status conference: set procedural schedule
- Temporary orders hearing (if needed): temporary custody/support/possession pending final resolution
- Discovery period: exchange documents, written questions, depositions if needed
- Mediation/settlement discussions: often required or recommended before trial
- Custody evaluation or expert work (if ordered)
- Pretrial conferences & resolution attempts
- Trial (if no settlement) and final decree
- Post-judgment modifications or enforcement as needed

Practical tips to prepare
- Bring a concise, dated chronology of major events (parenting time, incidents, financial changes) — 1–2 pages per major topic.
- Keep communications factual, save everything, and avoid posting case details on social media.
- Prioritize documents the judge will want quickly: income proof, child-related expenses, evidence about parenting and safety.
- Ask your attorney whether to file or oppose motions for temporary orders before the status conference.
- Know your goals (primary custody, shared custody, relocation plans, specific schedule) and your “bottom line” points for settlement.

Safety concerns and immediate steps
- If you or the children are unsafe: tell your lawyer immediately and ask about emergency orders and safety planning.
- Document any threats, abuse, or risky behavior in dated notes and preserve any evidence.

How I can help next
- Draft a concise email or list of questions to send to Robert Kim based on this list.
- Create a one-page chronology or checklist you can bring to the status conference.
- Prepare a template financial affidavit or memo of documents to hand to your lawyer.

If you want, I can:
- Draft an email to Robert Kim asking the key questions and requesting what docs he wants before the status conference, or
- Produce a one-page printable checklist/chronology template you can fill in.

Which would you like me to do next? And please confirm which court (Denver or Hennepin County) so I can adapt any local procedure suggestions.

