Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — this is a good moment to get organized. Below I’ve put together (A) a short checklist of clarifying questions to ask your attorney, (B) the typical next-step timeline in family-law cases and what to expect at a status conference, and (C) a practical documents/evidence checklist to prepare now. At the end I’ll ask two quick clarifying questions and offer to draft an email or one‑page summary to send to Robert Kim.

A. Key questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic)
- Case basics & jurisdiction
  - Where is the case actually filed? (You mentioned Denver and Hennepin County — which court is handling each issue?)
  - Who will handle this case day-to-day at the firm and how is best to contact them?
  - What is our overall case strategy (settlement vs trial)? What are the possible outcomes?

- Status conference & immediate next steps
  - What will happen at the status conference in two months? (Is it scheduling only, or will the judge consider temporary orders?)
  - What should I prepare/bring for that conference?
  - Are there any deadlines the court will set at the status conference (discovery deadlines, mediation, evaluation)?

- Custody / parenting time
  - What kind of custody/parenting-time arrangement should I request or oppose?
  - How is the court likely to evaluate the child’s best interests in this jurisdiction?
  - Will the court order a custody evaluation, guardian ad litem (GAL), or parenting coordinator? How do those work and who pays?
  - If relocation becomes an issue, what steps protect my rights or the child’s interests?

- Child support & finances
  - How will child support be calculated (income documentation needed, deviations from guidelines)?
  - If there are separate support/maintenance issues, how do they interact with custody?
  - What financial disclosures will be required and what is the timeline for exchanging them?

- Evidence, discovery & witnesses
  - What evidence is most important to our position (texts, emails, school records, medical/therapy notes, eyewitnesses)?
  - What discovery tools will we use (interrogatories, requests for production, subpoenas, depositions)?
  - Do we need expert witnesses (psychologist, financial expert)? If so, when should they be retained?

- Temporary/emergency orders
  - Should we request temporary custody, support, or other protective orders now?
  - What emergency options exist if there is an immediate safety/risk concern?

- Settlement, mediation & costs
  - Will the court require or encourage mediation? What is the mediation timeline and cost?
  - What are estimated attorney fees and other case costs going forward? How will billing work?
  - What settlement terms would be acceptable to you (child schedule, support floor/ceiling, parenting conditions)?

- Process, timeline & next milestones
  - What are the likely next milestones and realistic time estimates (temporary orders hearing, discovery completion, mediation, trial)?
  - What do I need to do now vs later?

- Communication & expectations
  - How often will you update me? What response time should I expect for messages?
  - How will we document and preserve communications with the other parent (if required)?

B. Typical next-step timeline and what to expect at the status conference
- Status conference (in ~2 months)
  - Usually used to confirm counsel, set a case schedule (deadlines for discovery, motions, mediation), and decide if temporary or emergency hearings are needed.
  - Sometimes the judge will set a temporary orders hearing date (for custody/support) or require mediation/parenting evaluation.

- Short-term (weeks–few months)
  - Exchange of initial disclosures/financial documents.
  - Possible temporary orders hearing if immediate custody/support is contested.
  - Mediation or settlement conference may be scheduled.

- Mid-term (3–9 months)
  - Discovery (document production, written questions, depositions) and any evaluations (custody evaluation, psychological).
  - Motions (e.g., motions to compel, motions for temporary relief).

- Long-term (6–18+ months)
  - Settlement negotiations; if no settlement, pretrial conference and trial.
  - After trial, orders (custody/support) and potential post-trial motions or appeals.

(Note: timelines vary widely by jurisdiction and case complexity. Your attorney will give more accurate local timing.)

C. Documents and evidence to gather now (practical checklist)
- Identification of parties & case materials
  - Any existing court orders, pleadings, prior agreements, parenting plans.
  - Court notices, summonses, filings.

- Financial documents (last 2–3 years as available)
  - Pay stubs, W-2s and last 2 years’ tax returns, 1099s.
  - Bank statements, credit-card statements, mortgage/lease documents.
  - Retirement account statements, investment accounts, business income statements.
  - Child care receipts, medical bills related to child, school expenses.

- Parenting & child documents
  - Child’s school records, attendance records, IEP/504 plans if applicable.
  - Medical/mental-health records for child if custody-related.
  - Documentation of parenting time: calendars, logs of exchanges, travel records.

- Communications & behavior evidence
  - Texts, emails, social-media messages relevant to custody or support.
  - Voicemails or call logs (if relevant and lawful to use).
  - Police reports, protective orders, CPS involvement records.

- Witnesses & third-party contacts
  - Names and contact info for witnesses (teachers, coaches, neighbors, babysitters).
  - Any experts you plan to consult (therapist, child psychologist, forensic accountant).

D. Practical steps you can take now
- Create a single chronological folder (digital & physical) for all case documents.
- Start a dated log of parenting time, incidents, important conversations.
- Save copies of all communications and court papers.
- If there are safety concerns, document and raise them with your attorney immediately.

E. Optional: I can help draft
- A short email to Robert Kim listing these questions and asking his guidance/what to prepare for the status conference.
- A one-page summary of your case facts to give the attorney before the conference.
- A file checklist or a template evidence log for you to fill out.

Two quick clarifying questions
1) Which court is handling this case? You mentioned both family court in Denver and Hennepin County, MN — which is the active filing for the custody/support matters? Jurisdiction affects process and timelines.
2) Do you want me to draft an email to Robert Kim now with the questions and a proposed checklist for the status conference?

Tell me which next step you want (draft email, one-page summary, or I can just give a compact printable checklist), and I’ll prepare it.

