Assistant: I can help — this is a good time to get organized. First, one quick clarification: you mentioned proceedings in Denver and also Hennepin County, Minnesota. Which court actually has the case (Denver or Hennepin County)? That affects process and local rules. Also tell me if there are any existing temporary orders, restraining orders, or immediate safety concerns.

Below is a practical, organized list you can use to prepare for your status conference and your meeting with Robert Kim. It includes: important legal terms you should know, suggested questions to ask your attorney, documents to gather, what typically happens next in custody/support cases, and immediate next steps you can take.

Key terms (MN-focused language where relevant)
- Allocation of parental responsibilities = legal decision-making authority (what many jurisdictions call “legal custody”).
- Parenting time = physical custody / visitation schedule.
- Child support = usually set by state guideline formulas (Minnesota uses guideline child support).
- Temporary orders = short-term custody/support/orders while the case is pending.
- Discovery = exchanging records, documents, depositions, interrogatories.
- Status conference = court meeting to set schedule, identify issues, and possibly narrow disputes.
- Mediation/alternative dispute resolution = required or recommended in many family courts before trial.
(Note: local names/processes vary — confirm with Robert Kim for Denver vs Hennepin County specifics.)

Suggested questions to ask your attorney (grouped)
Case basics & strategy
- What is our case theory or goal (what outcome are we aiming for on custody/support)?
- What are my rights as a parent in this jurisdiction (legal decision-making, parenting time presumptions)?
- How strong is our position based on what you’ve seen so far?
- What are the major risks and likely counterarguments from the other parent?

Status conference & short-term orders
- What should I expect at the status conference in two months? (e.g., scheduling orders, deadlines, temporary orders)
- Can we ask for temporary custody/support orders before then if needed?
- Should we seek emergency or temporary relief now (e.g., temporary parenting plan, temporary support)?

Custody & parenting time specifics
- How is parental responsibility decided here (factors the judge considers)?
- How are parenting time schedules typically set (weekend/holiday/school schedules)?
- What evidence do we need to support the custody/parenting time proposal we want?

Child support & finances
- How is child support calculated here and what financial documents are required?
- Should we seek temporary child support now? If so, how is that determined?
- Are spousal maintenance/alimony and asset division relevant? What financial disclosures will be required?

Discovery & evidence
- What documents do you want me to gather now (pay stubs, tax returns, bank statements, child-related expenses, school/medical records)?
- Should I keep a parenting journal or log communications/visitation? If so, what details matter most?
- Do we need witnesses, expert evaluations, or a custody evaluation/child forensic interview?

Settlement & ADR
- Is mediation required or strongly recommended in this court? When would that happen?
- What are acceptable settlement ranges for custody and support in your view?
- If settlement talks start, who will handle negotiations — you or me?

Procedures & timeline
- What are the likely next steps and approximate timeline from now to resolution (temporary orders, discovery period, mediation, pretrial, trial)?
- What deadlines will be set at the status conference that I need to be aware of?
- How long does typical discovery take and when might a custody evaluation be ordered?

Costs & billing
- What are estimated legal fees and costs (e.g., attorney fees, mediator, evaluator)? What is your billing structure?
- Can we limit costs by prioritizing issues or using limited discovery?

Practical & communication
- How will you communicate updates to me and how fast can I expect responses?
- What decisions require my explicit approval versus your discretion to handle?
- What should I say (or not say) to the other parent, on social media, to third parties, or to the children?

Documents and evidence checklist to gather now
- Court pleadings, petitions, summons, prior orders (if any)
- Any temporary or restraining orders
- Your ID, children’s birth certificates, school enrollment info and schedules
- Recent pay stubs, last 2–3 years’ tax returns, W-2s, 1099s
- Bank statements, retirement/asset statements, mortgage/lease, credit card statements
- Monthly household budgets and child-related expense receipts (daycare, medical, extracurricular)
- Communications with the other parent (texts, emails, parenting app logs) — preserve originals/screenshots
- Records showing parenting time history (calendars, photos showing dates, travel logs)
- Child medical and mental-health records, school records, IEPs or special services if applicable
- Any evidence of domestic violence, substance abuse, or concerns about safety (police reports, medical records)
- Witness names and short statements (friends, teachers, babysitters) if relevant

What typically happens next (common timeline)
- Pre-status: meet with attorney, gather documents, consider temporary relief if needed.
- Status conference: court sets deadlines (discovery cutoff, mediation date, expert deadlines), may set temporary orders or refer to mediation.
- Discovery: exchange financial info, depositions or written questions as needed.
- Mediation/settlement talks: try to reach agreement on parenting plan/support.
- Custody evaluations or expert involvement if disputed (parenting consults, mental health evaluations).
- Pretrial motions/hearings to resolve discrete issues.
- Trial (if settlement fails) where judge makes final decisions.
- Post-judgment: orders for enforcement or modification if circumstances change.

Practical tips for the status conference and your meeting with Robert Kim
- Bring the documents above and a concise written timeline of major events (incidents, parenting time problems, communications).
- Prepare a one-page statement of what you want (parenting schedule, decision-making, support) and why.
- Ask your lawyer to list the items you must do before the status conference (e.g., file financial affidavit).
- Confirm whether parents must attempt mediation and whether any evaluations are mandatory in this county.
- Ask for a calendar invite or confirmation of the status conference date/time and if remote attendance is allowed.

Safety & children’s welfare
- If there are any safety concerns (threats, violence, abuse, substance use), tell your attorney immediately — those issues can change strategy and the need for emergency orders.

Offer to help
- I can draft a clear meeting checklist or an email you can send to Robert Kim summarizing what you want to cover at your next appointment or asking specific procedural questions. Would you like me to draft that email or the one-page agenda for your status conference?
- Also tell me which court is actually handling the case (Denver or Hennepin County) so I can tailor procedure and local practice information.

If you want, give me a few details about what you want (primary custody? shared? current parenting time? any temporary orders now?), and I’ll build a tailored list of questions and a checklist you can use at your meeting.

