Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help you get organized and ready. Below is a practical, prioritized checklist of questions to ask Robert Kim, what to prepare and bring, and the typical next steps and timelines you should expect in a custody/support divorce matter. First, one quick clarification: you mentioned proceedings at family court in Denver and also Hennepin County, Minnesota. Which county/court is handling this matter? (Jurisdiction matters for timelines, rules, and forms — I can tailor this more precisely once you confirm.)

Questions to ask your attorney (prioritized)
- Confirm jurisdiction and venue: Which court is this actually in, and is there any risk of jurisdictional disputes?
- Status conference purpose & logistics: What is the date/time/location or Zoom link? What will we need to present at the status conference?
- Case strategy and goals: What outcome do you recommend on custody and support, and what are the realistic options (settlement vs litigation)?
- Temporary orders: Should we seek temporary custody, parenting time, or child support orders now? If so, what is the process and timeline?
- Discovery plan: What discovery will we need (documents, interrogatories, depositions, subpoenas)? Deadlines?
- Financial disclosure & support: What financial documents will you need to prepare child support, spousal support, and asset division analyses?
- Parenting evaluation / mediation: Is mediation or a custody evaluation likely/required here? What are the costs and timeline?
- Evidence & witnesses: What evidence or witnesses will strengthen our position (texts, emails, school records, witnesses)?
- Costs & timeline: Estimated timeline to resolution (settlement or trial) and estimated legal costs/fees for typical next steps.
- Communication & decision-making: How will we communicate (email, calls), and when will you need decisions from me?

Additional detailed questions you may want to ask
- If the other parent requests relocation, what are my rights and the factors the court weighs?
- How does the court calculate child support here (guidelines, deviations)?
- If we have immediate safety concerns, do I need an emergency order or protection order?
- What temporary parenting schedule is typical pending trial in this jurisdiction?
- Will you consider or request forensic accounting or vocational evaluation if income is disputed?
- If settlement negotiations occur, what are typical settlement components (parenting plan, support, custody/legal decision-making, tax exemptions, debt division)?
- What discovery responses will be handled by you vs. what I’ll prepare?
- What are likely court deadlines to expect after the status conference (e.g., disclosures, mediation, pretrial)?

What to bring / documents to gather before the status conference and for discovery
- Court filings you already have (petition, summons, temporary orders, any prior court orders)
- Attorney communications and retainer agreement with Robert Kim
- Parenting timeline/calendar: a chronological log of parenting time, major events, and any incidents
- Communications: relevant texts, emails, social media posts between you and the other parent (organized and dated)
- School records and IEPs, medical records for the child(ren), immunization records
- Employment & income: recent pay stubs (3–6 months), W-2s, tax returns (last 2–3 years), employer contact info
- Bank statements, credit card statements, mortgage/rent statements, retirement account statements
- Childcare, medical, school-related expenses receipts
- Any police reports, protective orders, or documentation of domestic incidents
- Witness names and short summaries (e.g., babysitter, teacher, family member)
- Any prior settlement offers or communications about custody/support

Typical next steps you can expect (general flow)
- Status conference: court will check case status, set a schedule, and may issue deadlines for discovery, mediation, or evaluations
- Exchange of disclosures: financial affidavits and document exchanges per local rules
- Mediation or settlement conferences: many family courts require or encourage mediation to try to resolve custody/support before trial
- Discovery: interrogatories, requests for production, depositions if necessary
- Temporary orders hearing (if requested): could set interim custody/times/support until final orders
- Parenting evaluation or custody study (if ordered): can take weeks/months and may influence final custody decisions
- Pretrial conference and then trial (if settlement not reached)
- Final judgment and post-judgment enforcement/modification steps

Practical preparation tasks for you now (in the next 2 months)
- Confirm and calendar the status conference date/time and how to appear
- Start collecting and organizing the documents above (scan and label files)
- Create a clear, concise timeline of parenting time and major events (one page summary)
- Note any urgent safety concerns and tell your attorney immediately
- Track all child-related expenses and make copies of receipts
- Save all communications with the other parent in one place, preserved and backed up
- Discuss with your attorney whether to propose temporary orders in advance

How to present things to your attorney for maximum efficiency
- Provide a one-page summary: goals, top concerns, important dates, immediate safety issues
- Provide the timeline and a folder (digital or physical) with labeled documents
- Highlight any urgent deadlines or prior court orders
- Ask your attorney to outline action items and who (you or lawyer) handles each

What the status conference itself usually looks like
- Short hearing (often 10–30 minutes) where the judge confirms service, sets a case schedule, and may order mediation/evaluations
- The judge may set deadlines for disclosures, motions, and a trial date window
- No final decisions on custody/support at this conference unless the parties have an agreed temporary order

Safety and parenting concerns
- If you feel unsafe at any time, tell your attorney immediately — emergency/temporary orders can be requested quickly
- Keep records of any threats, missed exchanges, or concerns about the child’s safety

Would you like me to:
- Draft an email to Robert Kim summarizing these questions and asking for a meeting or guidance before the status conference? (If yes, tell me any tone preference and any specific points to include.)
- Create a one-page timeline template for you to fill in?
- Produce a tailored checklist based on whether the case is in Denver or Hennepin County once you confirm the correct jurisdiction?

If you confirm the court location (Denver vs Hennepin County), I’ll tailor the checklist and mention any local rules or likely requirements specific to that jurisdiction.

