I can definitely help — below is a focused checklist of what to ask your attorney, what to prepare before the status conference, and a typical roadmap of next steps in a custody/support divorce matter. I’ll avoid giving legal advice; this is a preparation guide you can use with Robert Kim at Kim & Associates.

Quick context recap (so you and your attorney are on the same page)
- Attorney: Robert Kim, Kim & Associates
- Court: Family Court (you mentioned both Denver and Hennepin County — you noted the custody/support case is in Hennepin County, Minnesota)
- Next event: status conference in ~2 months
- Issues: custody (parenting/time), child support, and support/financial issues intertwined

1) High‑priority questions to ask your attorney (bring this list to your meeting)
- Case posture & strategy
  - What are my immediate rights and protections as a parent while this case proceeds?
  - What should my short‑term goals be (safety, custody/visitation, financial stability)?
  - What is your recommended overall strategy for custody and support here (settlement vs litigate)?
- Status conference specifics
  - What typically happens at the status conference in Hennepin County? What does the judge expect us to present?
  - What orders or scheduling decisions will likely be made at that conference?
  - What do you want me to prepare or present at that conference?
- Custody and parenting time
  - What types of custody (legal/physical) does Minnesota recognize and how are they decided?
  - What factors will the court consider in deciding custody and parenting time in our case?
  - What interim/temporary parenting time or restrictions should I request (or expect)?
  - How does relocation by either parent get handled?
- Child support and finances
  - How will child support be calculated here (income sources, imputed income, deviations)?
  - Should we seek temporary child support orders now? How and when are they requested?
  - What should we do about shared expenses, daycare, medical insurance and extraordinary expenses now?
- Spousal support / property division
  - If spousal maintenance is an issue, how is that determined and when should we address it?
  - What financial documentation will be needed for property/support division?
- Discovery, evidence, and experts
  - What discovery will be required (financial affidavits, interrogatories, depositions)?
  - Will we need a custody evaluation, parenting study, or expert (therapist, psychologist, CPA)? When should that be requested?
  - How should we preserve evidence (texts, emails, school/medical records, social media)?
- Timing, costs, and realistic outcomes
  - What is a realistic timeline from status conference to resolution or trial?
  - What are likely costs (attorney fees, expert fees) and options to manage them?
  - What settlement options are reasonable to pursue early on?
- Safety and enforcement
  - If there are safety concerns (abuse, harassment), what interim protective measures can the court order?
  - How are emergency or enforcement remedies handled if the other parent violates orders?
- Practical / administrative
  - Are there local court programs required (parenting education, mediation, conciliation) in Hennepin County?
  - What deadlines should I mark on my calendar (disclosures, motion cutoff, mediation)?
  - How often should we communicate and how do I send you documents securely?

2) Documents and information to gather now (get these organized and bring copies)
- Child-related: birth certificates, school records, daycare records, medical/mental health records, vaccination records, court or CPS records (if relevant)
- Communication: recent text messages, emails, social media messages between parents about custody/childcare, voicemails, calendars showing parenting time
- Financial: pay stubs (last 3–6 months), most recent W‑2s/1099s/tax returns (2–3 yrs), bank statements (3–6 months), credit card statements, mortgage/lease, retirement statements, proof of benefits/unemployment
- Expenses: receipts for childcare, medical, extracurriculars, insurance premiums, receipts showing extraordinary child expenses
- Legal filings: copies of the petition, answer, temporary orders (if any), prior court orders in any jurisdiction
- Witnesses: names/contact of people who can speak to parenting, home environment, or finances (teachers, doctors, family members)
- Safety documentation: police reports, medical records, text/email threats, protective order paperwork (if applicable)

3) What typically happens next (common roadmap & timeline)
- Before status conference (next 2 months)
  - Meet with your attorney to set goals and exchange initial documents
  - Draft and file any necessary temporary relief requests (temporary parenting plan, child support) if needed
  - Preserve and organize evidence; start any evaluations the attorney recommends
- Status conference
  - Judge usually sets a case schedule, deadlines for discovery, and may set a timeline for mediation/settlement conferences or evaluations
  - Parties often report on settlement efforts and requests for services (parenting classes, evaluations)
- Early case stage (1–4 months after status)
  - Exchange financial affidavits and initial discovery (interrogatories, document requests)
  - Mediation/conciliation or settlement conference often ordered/encouraged
  - If safety concerns exist, temporary orders for custody/visitation/support may be entered
- Mid-stage (3–9 months)
  - Completion of discovery, depositions if needed
  - Custody evaluation or parenting assessment if court orders
  - Negotiation of parenting plan and settlement terms; possible pretrial motions if disputes remain
- Trial or resolution (6–12+ months)
  - If no settlement, parties go to trial on custody/support/asset division; judge issues final order
  - Post‑trial motions or appeals may extend timeline
Note: Actual timing varies by court caseload, complexity, and willingness to negotiate.

4) How to be prepared for the status conference (practical tips)
- Ask your attorney for a short meeting about 2–3 weeks before the conference to review strategy.
- Prepare a one‑page chronology of major events (custody incidents, important dates).
- Bring proposed temporary parenting plan and child support figures (your suggestion for interim arrangements).
- Have copies of the key documents (financial affidavit, any pleadings, communications) ready to give to your attorney.
- Keep communication factual — avoid venting via email/texts that could be used in court.

5) Evidence & communication management
- Save and back up all communications (texts/screenshots, email threads). Keep originals when possible.
- Don’t delete messages even if emotional; preserve them.
- Limit one‑on‑one discussions with the other parent about legal terms — communicate through attorneys when possible.
- Document missed visits, late pickups, or other incidents with dates/times and witnesses.

6) Questions to ask specifically about your rights
- Am I entitled to physical custody or regular parenting time now?
- Can the other parent move the child out of county/state? What limitations exist?
- Do I have the right to request specific parenting time (overnights, holidays, exchanges)?
- What are my rights regarding my child’s medical, school, and extracurricular decisions?
- How do my rights change while temporary orders are in place vs after final orders?

7) Practical next actions you can take now (checklist)
- Schedule a meeting with Robert Kim as soon as possible — ask for a pre‑status conference planning session.
- Gather the documents listed above and create a folder (digital & physical).
- Create a concise timeline/chronology to hand your attorney (one page or bullet list).
- Note dates you are unavailable for meetings or court.
- Consider whether you want any protective orders, temporary child support, or temporary parenting time requests immediately.

8) Offer to help
I can:
- Draft a concise email to Robert Kim that lists these questions and requests a pre‑status conference meeting (draft only, or send if you want).
- Create a one‑page chronology template or a prioritized question sheet you can print.
- Help organize and label your documents into a checklist you can hand to your attorney.

Would you like me to draft the email to Robert Kim now (and if so, do you want it saved as a draft or sent)? If you want the draft, tell me any specifics you want included (dates/times you’re available, any urgent concerns, whether you want to request temporary orders).

