Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can definitely help you get organized and ready. Before I go into specifics, one quick clarification: you mentioned proceedings at family court in Denver and also said Hennepin County, MN. Which county/state is the case actually filed in (Denver, CO or Hennepin County, MN)? The local court rules and statutes (custody/support calculations, mediation, evaluations, emergency relief, etc.) depend on the forum, so I’ll tailor anything that needs local detail once you confirm.

Below is a focused, practical plan you can use right away: a prioritized list of questions to ask your attorney, the documents to bring to your meeting or status conference, what typically happens next in custody/support cases, and immediate steps you should take.

1) Key questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic)
- Case status / procedural
  - What is the purpose of the status conference and what will the judge decide at that hearing?
  - What deadlines will be set at the status conference (discovery, mediation, evaluations, motion deadlines, trial date)?
  - What are the next formal steps in this jurisdiction? (temporary orders, discovery, mediation, custody evaluation, trial)
  - Who will handle court filings and communications with opposing counsel?

- Jurisdiction & venue (ask especially if the county/state is unclear)
  - Is this the correct county to hear the case? Are there any jurisdictional issues we should be concerned about?
  - If either parent lives in a different state, how does that affect custody/support?

- Custody & parenting time
  - What standards does the court use to decide custody/parenting time in this jurisdiction?
  - What types of custody (legal, physical) and parenting schedules typically get ordered?
  - Do I have rights to sole or joint legal decision-making? What evidence would support either position?
  - How is relocation handled? What restrictions apply if the other parent wants to move?

- Child support & finances
  - How is child support calculated here (guidelines/formula)? What factors can change the guideline amount?
  - Will temporary child support be set before final orders?
  - How should we handle daycare, medical, extracurricular, and uncovered expenses?

- Temporary orders & emergencies
  - Should we seek temporary orders now (for custody, parenting time, support, exclusive use of the home, or protection orders)?
  - If there are safety concerns, what immediate protections are available?

- Discovery, evidence & evaluations
  - What evidence will be most important to the judge? (communications, school records, financials, photos, witness statements)
  - Will we need a custody evaluation or mental health evaluation? If so, who pays and how long does it take?
  - What is the process for exchanging documents and records with the other side?

- Settlement & mediation
  - Is mediation or settlement conference required in this county? At what stage?
  - What are our likely settlement options vs. litigating to trial? What’s your recommended strategy?
  - What is the realistic likelihood of settlement vs. trial based on similar cases?

- Costs & communication
  - What are your estimated fees and likely costs (expert evaluations, court reporters, private investigators)?
  - How will you communicate updates? What response time can I expect?
  - Are there tasks I can handle to reduce costs?

- Rights & long-term implications
  - What are my parental and financial rights during and after the proceedings?
  - If I agree to a temporary arrangement, how could that affect the final orders?
  - What can I do to preserve my rights and best position my case?

2) Documents and information to bring to your attorney or to the status conference
- Court papers already filed (complaint/petition, summons, any prior orders)
- Any temporary orders in effect
- Child/parent identifying info: birth certificates, school/enrollment, medical records
- Financial records (last 2 years preferred): pay stubs, W-2s/1099s, tax returns, bank statements, retirement/account statements, mortgage/lease, credit card statements, recent bills
- Childcare, daycare, extracurricular fee records and invoices
- Recent correspondence with the other parent (emails, texts, messages) — preserve originals
- Calendar or log of parenting time: who the child was with and when; any missed or denied visits
- Any records of incidents relevant to custody (police reports, protection orders, medical reports)
- Names/contact info for witnesses (family, teachers, neighbors)
- Any evaluation or mental/medical records for either parent or the child that are relevant

3) Immediate actions to take now (before the status conference)
- Clarify the correct forum (Denver vs. Hennepin County) and confirm hearing date/time/location.
- Preserve records: save texts, emails, voicemails, and take dated notes of important conversations or incidents.
- Start a parenting-time log (dates/times/notes).
- Gather the financial documents above; request employer verification if needed.
- Avoid negative or inflammatory posts about the other parent on social media.
- If safety is an issue, raise it now with your attorney so protective orders or emergency relief can be pursued.
- Consider whether you want/need temporary orders and ask your attorney to file or respond as needed.

4) What typically happens between now and a status conference / next steps after the conference
- Pre-conference: attorneys exchange basic info and propose scheduling orders; you may be asked to produce financial affidavits.
- Status conference: judge usually sets deadlines (discovery cutoff, mediation date, expert deadlines), may encourage settlement, and may issue temporary orders or schedule a temporary hearing.
- Post-conference: exchange of discovery (documents, interrogatories), mediation or settlement negotiation, possibly custody evaluation or home study, deposition or witness prep if going to trial, pretrial conference, and then trial if unresolved.
- If an agreement is reached at any stage, the judge can enter stipulated orders without trial.
