Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can help you get organized and ready. Quick clarification before I go further: you mentioned both "family court in Denver" and "Hennepin County, Minnesota." Which court/jurisdiction is this actually in? The steps and local rules differ by state/county, so confirm and I'll tailor the list more precisely.

Below is a practical checklist of what to ask your attorney, what typically happens next, and how to prepare for the status conference and the next stages of the case. I’ve organized it so you can use it as a meeting agenda or a checklist to bring to your attorney.

Priority questions to ask Robert Kim
- Case status & next hearing
  - What do you expect will happen at the status conference in two months? (typical court orders at that hearing)
  - Are there any immediate deadlines I should know about (discovery, motions, affidavits)?
- Temporary orders & immediate protections
  - Should we request temporary custody, child support, or spousal support orders before the status conference?
  - If the other parent is withholding the children or refusing visitation, what emergency steps can we take?
- Custody / parenting time (rights and likely outcomes)
  - How do Minnesota (or your jurisdiction) courts evaluate custody/parenting time? What factors matter most in our county?
  - What custody arrangement would the court likely consider in our situation? What outcome should I reasonably expect?
  - How do parental relocation requests work (if either parent might move)?
- Child support & financial issues
  - How is child support calculated here? What income/documents are required?
  - Will support be retroactive? How do we address imputed income or self-employment?
  - Are spousal maintenance/alimony issues relevant here?
- Discovery & evidence
  - What evidence will be most important for custody and support (financial records, communications, school/medical records)?
  - What is the discovery timeline and typical scope (depositions, interrogatories, financial disclosure)?
  - Should we preserve or subpoena records now (text messages, social media, childcare records)?
- Alternative dispute resolution and evaluations
  - Is mediation or a parenting evaluation likely? Will the court require parent education?
  - If a custody evaluation is ordered, how long does it take and what does it cost?
- Legal strategy & settlement
  - What’s our best strategy (settle vs. litigate)? What tradeoffs should I consider?
  - What are the likely costs and timeline to trial if we don’t settle?
  - What is your approach to negotiation and to communicating offers? How much authority do you want me to give to settle?
- Logistics & communications
  - How will you communicate case updates? (email, phone, client portal)
  - What are your billing rates, estimated costs, and how do retainers work?
  - Who else on your team will work on the case and how will I contact them?
- Practical next steps for me
  - Exactly which documents do you want me to gather before the status conference?
  - What should I do or avoid (e.g., communications with the other parent, social media activity)?

Typical next steps you can expect (general overview)
- Status conference: case management — judge usually sets schedule (deadlines for discovery, briefing, mediation or evaluations), and may address temporary orders.
- Temporary orders: parties often seek temporary parenting time, custody, child support, and/or temporary financial orders before final resolution.
- Discovery: exchange of financial documents, interrogatories, requests for production; possible depositions.
- ADR/mediation/parenting evaluation: many courts require mediation; a parenting evaluation or guardian ad litem may be ordered if custody is contested.
- Negotiation: most cases settle through negotiation or mediation before trial.
- Trial/Final hearing: if no settlement, a final hearing/trial resolves custody, parenting time, support, property division (if applicable).
- Post-judgment: enforcement, modification, or appeals later if circumstances change.

Documents and evidence to gather now
- Identification: birth certificates for children, marriage certificate, divorce/filing documents.
- Financial records (last 2–3 years if possible): pay stubs, W-2s, 1099s, tax returns, bank statements, investment accounts, retirement statements, mortgage/lease statements, credit card statements, loan documents, proof of other income.
- Monthly household budget / expenses: child care, school costs, medical expenses, extracurriculars.
- Communication records: text messages, emails, social-media messages relevant to parenting or safety; print or preserve them.
- Parenting-related: school records, medical records, IEPs, daycare invoices, schedules, childcare provider statements.
- Evidence of parenting / involvement: calendar of parenting time, photos, witnesses (family, daycare, teachers).
- Safety concerns: any police reports, protection orders, documentation of abuse or substance abuse incidents.
- Other: employment schedule, travel records, proof of residency.

How to prepare for the status conference (two-month timeline)
- Create a short written timeline of key events related to the children and dispute (dates, summary of incidents).
- Draft a proposed short parenting plan or your requested temporary orders (what schedule you want and why).
- Gather top-priority documents: current pay stubs, last year’s tax return, recent bank statements, and any safety-related documents.
- List witnesses and what they could testify to.
- Prepare 3–4 clear objectives you want from the status conference (e.g., get temporary support, get mediation ordered, set discovery deadlines).
- Ask your attorney whether you should file any motions in advance.

Things to avoid
- Don’t post negative commentary about the other parent or the case on social media.
- Don’t withhold children or violate existing court orders.
- Preserve evidence and avoid deleting communications that might be relevant.

Questions about costs, timeline, and settlement authority
- Ask for a realistic timeline from now to possible trial.
- Ask for an estimate of total costs for common paths (settlement vs. trial).
- Clarify how and when you should accept or reject settlement offers (get the attorney’s threshold and when to consult you).

Safety/emergency issues
- If there are immediate safety concerns for you or the children, say so now — your attorney can help pursue emergency orders quickly.
- Ask about temporary custody or protection orders if needed.

Offer to help
- If you’d like, I can:
  - Draft a concise email to Robert Kim listing these questions and asking for a meeting or guidance before the status conference.
  - Create a one-page status-conference agenda you can bring to your attorney or to court.
  - Make a checklist (with document labels) you can use to collect records.
  - Help organize a timeline of events from notes or messages you have.

Next step from you
- Please confirm the correct jurisdiction (Hennepin County, Minnesota or Denver/family court) and whether you want me to draft an email to Robert Kim or prepare a printable checklist/agenda for your meeting/status conference. Also tell me if there are any immediate safety concerns.

