I can help — this is a good time to get organized. First, a quick clarification: you mentioned family court in Denver and Hennepin County, Minnesota. Which court/location is the upcoming status conference in? Jurisdiction matters for timing, forms, and how child support/custody are decided. Once you confirm that, I’ll tailor anything jurisdiction-specific.

Below is a practical, prioritized plan: key questions to ask Robert Kim, a checklist of documents to gather, what typically happens next (including what to expect at the status conference), and immediate actions you can take in the two-month window.

1) Quick list of priority clarifying questions to ask your attorney (bring these to your meeting or email them)
- Which court has jurisdiction in this case (Denver/Colorado or Hennepin County/Minnesota)? Does either side need to file anything about jurisdiction or venue?
- What are my immediate rights and options regarding custody and parenting time while the case is pending?
- Are there existing temporary orders (custody, support, restraining) or do we need to request temporary/emergency relief?
- How is child support calculated in our jurisdiction and what documents will be necessary to prepare an accurate worksheet?
- What should I expect at the status conference? What will the judge likely decide or schedule?
- What deadlines will the court likely set (discovery cutoff, mediation, evaluations, motion deadlines, trial date)?
- Do you recommend mediation, custody evaluation, parenting coordinator, or expert witnesses (therapist, forensic accountant)?
- What discovery will we need (financial disclosure, communications, school/medical records)? Who will serve discovery and when?
- What are the likely costs (your fees, expert fees, mediation) and a realistic timeline to resolution?
- If there are safety concerns (domestic violence, substance abuse), what immediate steps should I take to protect myself/children?
- How will relocation requests be handled if one parent wants to move?
- What outcome should I prepare for and what compromise positions should I consider?
- How will we communicate? What’s the best way to reach you and how quickly can I expect responses?

2) Documents and information to gather now (start assembling these)
- Court filings you already have (petition, response, temporary orders, notices)
- Dates: hearing/status conference date, deadlines, any prior court dates
- Parenting history: current parenting schedule, childcare, school info, extracurriculars
- Child records: school reports, IEPs, medical records, therapy notes
- Communications: text messages, emails, social-media messages relevant to custody/support
- Financial records for you and, if available, the other parent:
  - Recent pay stubs (last 2–3 months)
  - Last 2 years of tax returns (personal & business, if applicable)
  - Bank statements (last 6–12 months)
  - Retirement statements, investment accounts
  - Mortgage/rent, utilities, insurance payments, childcare costs, medical bills
  - Credit card/debt statements
  - Proof of other income (bonuses, commissions, unemployment, benefits)
- Evidence of parenting involvement: calendars, visitation logs, caregiver receipts, photos showing involvement (if relevant)
- Any police reports, protection orders, or prior family court orders involving either parent
- List of potential witnesses (family, teachers, coaches, therapists)

3) What typically happens next and what the status conference usually accomplishes
- Status conference purpose: the judge reviews case progress and issues a schedule. Expect the court to:
  - Confirm or set deadlines for discovery (exchange of documents)
  - Order or refer parties to mediation or settlement conference
  - Set dates for custody evaluation or parenting-time assessments if requested
  - Set deadlines for filing motions and a possible trial date window
  - Address temporary orders (if asked) or confirm existing temporary arrangements
- Typical next steps after status conference:
  - Exchange financial disclosures and parenting plans
  - Mediation (often required before trial)
  - Discovery (interrogatories, document requests, depositions)
  - Custody evaluation or child specialist appointment if the court orders it
  - Pretrial motions or settlement negotiations
  - If unresolved, a trial or evidentiary hearing

4) Strategy items to discuss with your attorney
- Your objectives (primary concerns, what you will and won’t compromise on)
- Whether to pursue temporary orders now (custody, support, exclusive use of home, restraining order)
- Whether to ask for a custody evaluation or suggest mediation first
- Evidence plan — what we will present (documents, witnesses, experts)
- Settlement posture and negotiation strategy (what you’ll offer/accept)
- Parenting plan proposal to present at the status conference (clear, reasonable schedule)
- Fees and cost management — discuss budgeting and whether alternative dispute resolution could reduce cost/time

5) Practical things to prepare before the status conference (in the next two months)
- Create a simple timeline of events and parents’ interactions relevant to custody/support
- Draft a proposed parenting plan (regular schedule, holidays, vacations, decision-making, transportation)
- Prepare a financial snapshot and fill out any financial affidavit or child support worksheet the attorney provides
- Start organizing and redacting documents to share with opposing counsel (per attorney guidance)
- If safety is a concern, document incidents and consider filing for protective orders early
- Consider arranging a neutral evaluator or beginning an assessment if your attorney recommends it
- Keep a concise log of parenting time and communication for the record

6) Safety, emergencies, and urgent relief
- If children or you are at risk, ask your attorney about emergency (ex parte) orders or immediate protective relief
- Preserve and back up evidence (screenshots, police reports, medical records)
- Limit direct conflict with the other parent; communicate through counsel if advised

7) Jurisdictional / interstate issues (if applicable)
- If one parent lives or intends to move to another state, ask about the Uniform Child Custody Jurisdiction and Enforcement Act (UCCJEA) and how it affects filings, relocation, and enforcement
- If the case might involve Denver vs. Hennepin County, your attorney must confirm which court is proper and whether motions to transfer or contests over jurisdiction are needed

8) Sample agenda for your meeting with Robert Kim
- Confirm court/jurisdiction and status conference logistics
- Review timeline of events and any immediate safety concerns
- Walk through rights and likely temporary orders
- Present your parenting plan and financial snapshot
- Ask what the judge will be looking for at the status conference
- Confirm a concrete checklist and deadlines for documents and discovery
- Agree on communication expectations and next call/meeting

9) Offer to help
- I can draft a concise email to Robert Kim summarizing these questions and asking for guidance before the status conference, or draft the parenting plan and a financial checklist for you to review with him. Which would you prefer?
- Also tell me which court the status conference is in (Denver or Hennepin County) and the status conference date, and I’ll tailor the checklist and timeline to your jurisdiction.

If you want, tell me any immediate concerns (safety issues, temporary orders already in place, date of the conference, whether the other parent lives in another state) and I’ll draft the email to your attorney or create a one-page checklist you can bring to the meeting.

