This is a good time to get organized — I can help you make a focused list so your meeting with Robert Kim is efficient and you’re ready for the status conference. Before I dive in: you mentioned both “family court in Denver” and “Hennepin County, Minnesota.” Which is the correct court? Venue matters for procedure and deadlines, so please confirm.

Below is a practical plan: priority questions to ask your attorney, documents to gather, a typical timeline of next steps in a custody/support case, what to expect at the status conference, and some suggested phrasing you can use when you speak with Robert. If you’d like, I can draft an email to Robert summarizing these points and asking for a prep meeting.

1) Priority issues / questions to ask Robert Kim (short, high-impact)
- Confirm jurisdiction and venue: Are we definitely in Hennepin County (MN) or Denver (CO)? If Hennepin, is the file assigned to family court?  
- Case status & deadlines: What will be addressed at the status conference in two months? Are there any filings or discovery I must complete before then?  
- Temporary orders: Should we request temporary custody, parenting time, child support, spousal support, or emergency orders now? If so, how and when?  
- Parenting time and custody basics: What custodial arrangement does the law presume? What are my rights for physical custody, legal custody, school/medical decisions?  
- Child support: How is child support calculated here? Will we use guideline calculators, and what inputs do you need?  
- Financial disclosures: What financial documents must we exchange and by when (tax returns, pay stubs, bank/retirement statements, debts)?  
- Discovery plan: What discovery do you recommend (interrogatories, document requests, depositions)? What’s the timeline?  
- Custody evaluation / GAL: Is a custody evaluator or guardian ad litem likely or required? If recommended, what is the process and cost?  
- Mediation/alternative dispute resolution: Is mediation required/available? When should we try to settle vs. prepare for trial?  
- Evidence & witnesses: What evidence and witnesses will be strongest for custody/support (texts, school/medical records, witness statements)?  
- Relocation: If relocation or travel is likely, what is required to change a child’s residence legally?  
- Safety concerns: If there are safety or domestic violence issues, should we file a protective order now?  
- Fees & timeline: What is our estimated timeline to resolution and an estimate of legal fees for the next 3 months? Any cost-saving steps?  
- Communication & billing: Best way to contact you, and how will billing be handled (retainer, hourly, estimate)?

2) Documents and information to collect before meeting (bring originals and copies)
- Children: names, birthdates, schools, daycare, doctors, special needs, schedules.  
- Parenting records: calendar of parenting time, exchanges, travel, incidents; any concerns about the other parent (dates, short descriptions).  
- Communication logs: texts, e-mails, call logs relevant to custody or support.  
- Court papers: petition, answer, temporary orders, existing custody/support orders, service paperwork.  
- Financials: last 2–3 years of federal tax returns, recent pay stubs (3–6 months), W-2s/1099s, bank statements (last 6–12 months), retirement and investment statements, mortgage/loan statements, property deeds, vehicle titles, credit-card statements.  
- Household expenses: itemized monthly child expenses (childcare, medical, extracurriculars).  
- Employment: employer contact info, work schedule, history of income fluctuations.  
- Evidence: relevant photos, school/medical reports, police reports, witness contact info.

3) Typical next steps/timeline in custody/support matters (what usually happens)
- Initial pleadings: petition and answer (likely already filed).  
- Status conference: judge checks progress, sets deadlines for discovery, mediation, evaluations, and trial date window.  
- Temporary orders/hearings: either by agreement or motion if urgent temporary custody/support needed.  
- Financial disclosure & discovery: exchange of financial affidavits, documents, written discovery.  
- Mediation/conciliation: many courts require or strongly encourage mediation before trial.  
- Custody evaluation / GAL appointment (if ordered): evaluator interviews parents/children, observes, and issues report.  
- Motions & hearings: either party can bring motions (e.g., contempt, modification, temporary relief).  
- Settlement negotiations or trial: if no settlement, move to trial and final orders.  
- Post-judgment: enforcement or modifications later if circumstances change.

4) Specific custody/support questions to include (phrased for the attorney)
- “Based on the facts I’ve given you, what temporary parenting schedule should we ask the court to order right now?”  
- “How will the court likely calculate child support in my case? What numbers should we prepare?”  
- “If the other parent refuses to cooperate with discovery or refuses to follow exchanges, what remedies can you seek?”  
- “Would you recommend a custody evaluation or a guardian ad litem in this case? Pros/cons and costs?”  
- “What degree of evidence (texts, witnesses, records) do we need to establish our position on custody and support?”  
- “If there are safety concerns, what immediate filings should we make?”  
- “What steps can I take now to protect my parental rights and strengthen my case?”

5) What to expect at the status conference
- The judge will usually ask whether parties have exchanged documents, whether mediation or evaluations are needed, and will set a schedule (deadlines for discovery, filings, possible trial window).  
- The judge may encourage settlement and may set a date for a future hearing.  
- You generally do not argue substantive custody issues at a status conference — it’s about scheduling and case management — unless there’s an emergency.

6) Practical next steps you can do now (before your meeting)
- Confirm which court/venue and send that to Robert if he hasn’t already clarified.  
- Start gathering documents above and create a simple timeline of parenting events (who did what, when).  
- Save and organize all text/message logs and label them by date.  
- Make a short written summary of what outcome you want (specific parenting plan, support level, schooling wishes).  
- Decide whether you want to pursue temporary orders now or wait until the status conference. Ask Robert which he recommends.

7) Safety, relocation, and special circumstances (if applicable)
- If safety is a concern, tell your attorney immediately and consider seeking emergency protective or restraining orders.  
- If you or the other parent plans to move out of state or relocate with the child, ask how to seek permission or oppose relocation.

8) Suggested closing / administrative items to ask your attorney
- “Can you give me a short checklist of the documents you need and the dates they’re due?”  
- “Can you estimate costs for discovery, evaluations, mediation, and a trial?”  
- “Would you meet or do a phone call one month before the status conference to confirm strategy?”

9) Offer to help
- I can draft a concise email to Robert Kim summarizing these questions and requesting a prep meeting — would you like me to draft that now?  
- If you prefer, I can turn the list above into a one-page checklist you can bring to meetings or a script you can read during calls.

Finally — quick clarifying questions so I can tailor the next steps:
- Which court is handling the case (Hennepin County, MN or Denver, CO)?  
- Ages of the children and whether there are any immediate safety issues or existing court orders?  
- Would you like me to draft the email to Robert and/or a one-page checklist for your meeting?

Tell me which of those next steps you want me to do (draft email, make checklist, or answer anything else) and confirm the court.

