Thanks — I can help you get organized. Before I do the checklist and next-step plan, one quick clarification so my suggestions fit your case: you mentioned a family court in Denver and also Hennepin County, Minnesota. Which jurisdiction is this actually in (Denver/Colorado or Hennepin County/Minnesota)? Also, is Robert Kim (Kim & Associates) your attorney on the case in that jurisdiction?

Below is a focused checklist you can use to prepare for the status conference and for a meeting with your attorney, plus common next steps in custody/support cases and suggested questions to ask Robert Kim.

Key questions to confirm with your attorney (ask these first)
- Which court has jurisdiction (Denver/CO or Hennepin County/MN)? If there’s confusion, how do we resolve it?
- Who is our primary contact at Kim & Associates and how do you prefer to communicate (email, phone, secure portal)?
- What is our immediate objective for the status conference in two months?
- Are there any temporary orders in place now? If not, should we request temporary custody/parenting time/support now?
- What are my rights regarding custody, parenting time, relocation, and decision-making for the child under applicable state law?
- What facts or evidence will strengthen my position? What facts could harm it?
- What deadlines should I be aware of (discovery, disclosures, motions, mediation, pretrial)?
- What is the likely timeline from now to trial if no settlement is reached?
- What are the likely costs (attorney fees, experts, evaluations) and do we need a budget or fee agreement update?
- Should we consider mediation, a custody evaluation, or other assessments? When and how are those scheduled?
- What documents and financial information do you need from me right away?
- Are there safety or emergency concerns that warrant a protective or emergency order?
- If the other parent is not complying with court orders, what enforcement options do we have?
- What strategy do you recommend for settlement vs. taking the case to trial?

Documents and evidence to gather (start collecting these now)
- Child-related: birth certificate, school records, daycare info, medical records, immunizations, therapists/providers, IEPs or special-education documents
- Parenting evidence: proposed schedules, calendar of time with child, communication logs/texts/emails with the other parent, documented incidents or concerns, witness names
- Safety: police reports, restraining orders, photos, medical records of injuries
- Financial: last 2–3 years of tax returns, recent pay stubs (3–6 months), W-2s, 1099s, bank and investment statements, mortgage/rent, credit card statements, loan info, retirement account statements, proof of daycare costs, health insurance costs
- Household: lease/mortgage, utility bills, proof of residence
- Child support specifics: current childcare costs, healthcare premiums and out-of-pocket medical expenses, day care receipts, school tuition, extraordinary medical expenses

What to expect at the status conference (typical purposes)
- Court confirms the schedule for the case (deadlines for discovery, motion practice, mediation, and trial date windows)
- The judge may encourage/require mediation or settlement efforts
- Temporary orders may be requested or modified (custody, parenting time, child support, child expenses)
- The judge often asks whether parties are ready to proceed to trial and whether they need more time
- The court may set deadlines for disclosures and any expert evaluations

Typical next steps in custody/support cases
- Exchange of mandatory financial disclosures and parenting proposals
- Temporary orders hearing (if urgent relief needed before final hearing)
- Discovery (written questions, document requests, depositions)
- Mediation or settlement conferences (often required before trial)
- Child custody evaluation (if court orders an evaluator/guardian ad litem)
- Pretrial motions (e.g., to exclude evidence, for contempt, for temporary relief)
- Final hearing/trial (judge decides custody/support if no settlement)
- Post-judgment enforcement or modification proceedings (later, if circumstances change)

Good, specific questions about custody and parenting time
- What custody arrangement (legal decision-making and physical custody) should I seek given my situation?
- How is parenting time typically structured in this county for our child’s age?
- If I want to relocate (or the other parent wants to), what restrictions or notice requirements apply?
- How do we handle disputes over schooling, religion, medical treatment, and extracurriculars?
- Can temporary orders address school enrollment and daycare decisions now?

Child support and financial questions
- How will child support be calculated in this jurisdiction? What income counts (bonuses, self-employment, imputed income)?
- How are daycare, medical insurance, and extraordinary medical/educational expenses allocated?
- What documentation do we need to support requested child support or deviations from guideline amounts?

Discovery, evidence, and witnesses
- What specific discovery should we request from the other parent? (financial records, communications, parenting logs)
- Are text messages, emails, and social media admissible? How should we preserve them?
- Which witnesses should we consider (teachers, physicians, family members)?
- Should we anticipate needing a custody evaluator or guardian ad litem?

Settlement strategy and negotiation
- What is a reasonable opening settlement position and what are likely compromise points?
- What are our “deal-breakers” and what concessions can we realistically make?
- Are there negotiation tactics your attorney recommends (early mediation, presenting a parenting plan)?

Practical items and prep for the status conference
- Prepare a one-page case summary for your attorney: key facts, desired outcome, safety concerns, and immediate asks.
- Draft a proposed parenting plan (even a rough version) to show the court you’re prepared to co-parent.
- Create a timeline of major incidents and key dates.
- Prepare an initial set of questions for opposing-party discovery.
- Bring originals and copies of key documents to the conference.

If there are safety concerns or domestic violence
- Tell your attorney immediately. Ask about emergency protective orders, supervised exchanges, or supervised visitation.
- Preserve any threatening messages and document dates/times of incidents.

Costs and timing to discuss with your lawyer
- Ask for an estimate of total legal costs for interim steps (motions, mediation) and a range for trial.
- Discuss cost-saving options: limited-scope representation, targeted discovery, early mediation, or alternative dispute resolution.

Next actions I can help you with right now
- Draft a concise email or list of questions to Robert Kim asking the items above (I can draft it in a tone you prefer).
- Create a one-page case summary template you can fill in and bring to your attorney.
- Build a document checklist (fillable) so you can gather everything quickly.

Would you like me to draft an email to Robert Kim now with these questions and a short case summary asking for guidance ahead of the status conference? If so, tell me:
- Which jurisdiction is correct (Denver/CO or Hennepin County/MN)?
- Do you want a brief email (one-paragraph) or a detailed email including the full list above?

