Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help you get organized and prepared. Below is a practical checklist: specific questions to ask Robert Kim, documents to gather, what typically happens next (including what to expect at the status conference), immediate steps you can take, and things to avoid. First — one quick clarification I noticed: you mentioned family court in Denver and also Hennepin County, Minnesota. Which court actually has the case? Jurisdiction matters for procedures and law; if you’re not sure, mention that to your attorney right away.

Questions to ask your attorney (grouped and with suggested wording)
- Jurisdiction & case basics
  - Which court has jurisdiction (Denver or Hennepin County)? If multiple filings exist, how does that affect the case?
  - What is the judge’s role in the upcoming status conference and what decisions can be made there?

- Custody / parenting time (legal and physical custody)
  - What types of custody orders are possible in my situation (legal custody, physical custody, sole vs. joint)?
  - What immediate rights do I have as a parent while the case proceeds?
  - How are parenting time schedules usually handled while the case is pending? Can we propose a temporary parenting plan?
  - If the other parent wants to relocate (or I plan to relocate), how will that affect custody/parenting time?

- Child support & financial issues
  - How will child support be calculated (guidelines used by the court)? What documents do you need from me to calculate support?
  - Can we seek temporary support orders now? If so, how quickly can they be put in place?
  - How do shared costs (health insurance, daycare, medical expenses) get allocated while the case is pending?

- Immediate procedural steps & status conference
  - What is the purpose of the status conference in two months? What does the judge typically expect us to have done by then?
  - Will the court set deadlines (discovery, mediation, evaluations) at that conference?
  - Do we need to request temporary orders before the status conference? If yes, what evidence is required?

- Discovery, evaluations & evidence
  - What discovery should we start now (financial affidavits, school records, medical records, communications)?
  - Will the court order a child custody evaluation, guardian ad litem (GAL), or parenting coordinator? When is that appropriate?
  - How do we get records (medical, school, counseling) subpoenaed if necessary?

- Strategy & settlement
  - What are realistic goals and likely outcomes for custody and support in this case?
  - Do you recommend mediation or settlement discussions before trial? What are the pros/cons?
  - What would push you to try the case rather than settle?

- Practical & tactical
  - Who will be my primary contact at the firm and what is the best way to communicate (email, phone)?
  - Estimated timeline to resolution (short, medium, long term) and likely costs/fees. What is your billing structure and retainer?
  - What is the plan for handling exchanges, drop-offs, or supervised visits if tensions are high?

- Enforcement & emergency protections
  - If the other parent violates an order, how do we enforce it? How quickly can we get relief?
  - If there are safety concerns for me or the child, how do we request emergency/temporary protective orders?

Documents to gather now
- Identification and court paperwork: any current/previous pleadings, summons, temporary orders
- Financials: last 2–3 years of tax returns, recent pay stubs, W-2s/1099s, bank statements, mortgage/lease, credit card and loan statements, retirement account statements, business income statements (if applicable)
- Child-related: children’s birth certificates, school records (attendance, IEPs), medical records, vaccination records, daycare receipts, child-related receipts (medical, extracurricular)
- Communications: copies/screenshots of texts, emails, social media messages relevant to custody/support/exchanges (keep originals/metadata if possible)
- Safety/behavioral: police reports, CPS reports, photos, medical records, witness contact info if there are safety issues
- Housing & work: current address, work schedule, childcare arrangements, proof of who primarily cares for children (calendars, childcare invoices)

What usually happens next (typical timeline and processes)
- Before the status conference (weeks leading up)
  - You and your attorney exchange initial disclosures and financial affidavits
  - Gather documents and create chronology of events (timeline of parenting events, incidents)
  - Consider filing or responding to requests for temporary orders (parenting time, support, exclusive use of home)
- Status conference (the judge’s goals)
  - Confirm parties and counsel, set a case schedule (deadlines for discovery, motion cutoffs)
  - The judge may encourage or require mediation/alternate dispute resolution
  - Court may set deadlines for evaluations (custody evaluation/GAL) and set a trial date window or status check dates
- Middle phase (1–6 months after)
  - Exchange discovery (interrogatories, document production)
  - Attend mediation or settlement conferences; possibly custody evaluation or GAL appointment
  - Motions practice if temporary orders or discovery disputes arise
- Pre-trial & trial (6+ months)
  - Final pretrial motions, witness lists, trial briefs, exhibits
  - Trial or settlement agreement reached beforehand
  - Post-judgment enforcement and possible appeals

Immediate actions you can take now
- Confirm which county/court handles the case and tell your lawyer if you’re unsure
- Begin collecting the documents listed above (especially recent pay stubs, tax returns, and communications)
- Create a dated timeline of parenting-related events and any incidents relevant to custody/support
- Preserve evidence: save screenshots, emails, and back up voicemails; don’t delete anything relevant
- Avoid unilateral changes to parenting time or moving the children without court approval
- Limit direct contentious communication with the other parent — keep interactions documented and professional; consider using a neutral exchange app if ordered

Things to avoid
- Don’t post about the case or the children on social media
- Don’t withhold the children from the other parent unless there is an immediate safety risk and you’ve consulted your attorney
- Don’t destroy or alter documents or communications that could be evidence
- Don’t make promises about relocation, finances, or custody arrangements without discussing with your attorney

Questions about rights you should raise specifically
- What immediate parental rights do I retain while the case is pending?
- Can the other parent unilaterally restrict my parenting time or decision-making?
- If there are concerns about safety, what is the standard to obtain emergency restrictions?
- What rights do I have regarding medical and educational decision-making while temporary orders are in effect?

Preparation for the status conference (practical checklist)
- Ask your attorney if they will attend and whether you need to appear
