Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help you get organized for the next steps and for your meeting with Robert Kim. First — quick clarification: you mentioned proceedings at family court in Denver and also said Hennepin County, Minnesota. Which court/location is actually handling the case? That changes some local rules and timing. Also tell me if there are any existing temporary orders (custody/child support/ restraining orders) or any immediate safety concerns.

Below is a focused, practical plan you can use right away: (1) a prioritized list of questions to ask your attorney, (2) a documents checklist to gather before the status conference or your next meeting, (3) a typical timeline and next steps in family court custody/support cases, and (4) practical tips and things to avoid. Tell me if you want this turned into an email to Robert Kim or a printable checklist.

1) Key questions to ask your attorney (prioritized)
- Case status & strategy
  - What is our overall legal strategy and goal for custody and support?
  - What do you expect will happen at the status conference in two months?
  - Are there immediate deadlines or filings I need to be aware of?
  - What are the real strengths and weaknesses of my position?
- Custody / parenting time (parental responsibilities in MN)
  - What custody arrangement (parental responsibilities) should I seek and why?
  - What parenting-time schedule should I propose as temporary and long-term?
  - How do Minnesota (or the correct state’s) “best interests of the child” factors apply to my situation?
  - If the other parent is asking for more time or full custody, what evidence will be persuasive?
  - Do we need a custody evaluation or parenting study? When should it be requested?
- Child support & finances
  - How will child support be calculated here? Can you run the guideline worksheet for my case?
  - How are spousal support/alimony issues handled (if applicable)?
  - Do we need temporary support orders now (support or expense sharing)?
- Temporary orders & emergency relief
  - Should we ask the court for temporary custody, parenting time, support, or exclusive use of the home before trial?
  - If there are safety concerns, do we need emergency or protective orders?
- Discovery & evidence
  - What documents and records do we need to exchange or file (financial affidavits, tax returns, pay stubs)?
  - Do we need text logs, emails, social media posts, witnesses, school or medical records?
  - How should we authenticate digital evidence (texts, social media)?
- Mediation, settlement, and trial
  - Is mediation or settlement conference required/typical here? When will it occur?
  - What are realistic settlement options and a fallback plan if mediation fails?
  - How long until trial, and what is the trial process like in this court?
- Costs, timeline, and communication
  - What are anticipated costs (your fees, expert witness fees, evaluator fees)?
  - How will you communicate updates and how quickly can I expect responses?
  - What decisions do you want me to make and when?
- Practical / administrative
  - What should I say (or not say) to the other parent or third parties?
  - Is there anything I should avoid doing that could hurt the case?

2) Documents & evidence to gather now
Financial documents
- Most recent pay stubs (3 months), W-2s and tax returns (last 2–3 years)
- Recent bank account statements, investment statements, retirement account statements
- Mortgage/lease, utility bills, car payments, credit card statements, other debt documentation
- Childcare, school, medical and therapy expense records and invoices
Custody & parenting evidence
- School records, medical records for the child, dental/therapy invoices
- A calendar/log of parenting time (who had the child and when), notes of missed visitations or incidents
- Communications: texts, emails, social media messages relevant to custody/support (export/save them; screenshots with timestamps)
- Any police reports, protective-order paperwork, or documented incidents
Other useful docs
- Prior or existing court orders, pleadings, petitions, responses, and any custody or support worksheets
- A list of potential witnesses and short summary of what they would testify about
- Work schedules, travel schedules, planned relocation details (if relocation might be an issue)
Organize these in clear folders (paper or digital) with date ranges.

3) Typical next steps & approximate timeline (what often happens)
- Status conference (in two months): judge typically sets a schedule (deadlines for discovery, motions, mediation/conciliation, possible hearing dates). Be prepared to present short proposals for temporary orders or a timeline.
- Scheduling order: sets deadlines for exchange of financial affidavits, disclosures, expert reports, and dates for mediation/conciliation or settlement conferences.
- Temporary orders hearing (if requested): for temporary custody, parenting time, child support, spousal support, or exclusive-use orders.
- Mediation/conciliation: many family courts require or strongly encourage mediation to attempt settlement before trial.
- Discovery and financial disclosure: exchange of tax returns, paystubs, financial affidavits, deposition or interrogatory planning.
- Evaluations (if ordered): custody/parenting evaluations or mental health assessments can take additional months.
- Settlement negotiations or pretrial motions: parties often settle before trial; if not, preparation for trial (exhibits, witness prep).
- Trial: final determination on custody and support if no settlement. Trials can be scheduled many months out depending on the court backlog.
Timeframe: from filing to final trial can be several months to over a year depending on complexity, need for evaluations, and local backlog. The status conference typically sets the schedule.

4) Custody and child support specifics you should ask about
- Request that your attorney run the state child support guideline worksheet and show how different parenting-time splits affect support.
- Ask when to request a parenting/forensic evaluator and how much that typically costs here.
- Ask what temporary custody proposals are reasonable and what the court tends to order in similar cases.
- If relocation is likely (either parent), ask how the court handles proposed moves and required notice.

5) Practical tips & things to avoid
- Don’t delete texts, emails, or social media messages related to the case—preserve everything.
- Avoid posting about the case on social media or criticizing the other parent publicly.
- Keep interactions with the other parent documented and civil; if you must communicate, prefer written forms (text/email).
- Don’t introduce the child to new romantic partners until the case is resolved, if it could be used against you.
- If safety is an issue, raise that with your attorney immediately—don’t wait.

6) Ask your attorney to prepare or confirm these specific items before the status conference
- A recommended temporary parenting-time plan and proposed temporary support order to file with the court
- A proposed scheduling order or timeline (discovery deadlines, mediation date, evaluation request)
- A list of documents they’ll file or need from you and dates when they must be submitted
- Whether the court requires mediation/conciliation and when to expect it
- An estimate of costs for discovery, experts, and likely timeline to trial if no settlement

7) Offer I can do for you now
- Draft a concise email to Robert Kim listing your questions and attaching the documents checklist (I can draft it for you).
- Create a printable meeting checklist/agenda for your session with your attorney.
- Help you gather and organize the specific documents or export messages in a clear format.
- Prepare a short script or bullet points for what to say at the status conference (if you plan to attend).

Next step from me: confirm which court (Denver or Hennepin County, MN) and whether you want me to draft the email to Robert Kim or a one-page meeting checklist. Also tell me if you have any urgent concerns (safety, existing orders, upcoming hearings) so I can prioritize.

