Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
First, one quick clarification: you mentioned family court in Denver and also Hennepin County, Minnesota. Which court is actually handling the case? Jurisdiction affects procedure and which local rules apply — I can tailor this guidance once you confirm.

Below is a practical, organized plan you can use to prepare for the status conference and your meetings with Robert Kim. It includes suggested questions to ask, a documents checklist, a short timeline of typical next steps in family/custody cases (with Minnesota notes where relevant), immediate actions you can take now, and a ready-to-send email template to your attorney asking for a pre-conference meeting.

1) Key questions to ask your attorney (prioritize these at your meeting)
- Confirm basics and jurisdiction
  - Which court has jurisdiction — Denver or Hennepin County? Does that change any deadlines or strategy?
  - Who will attend the status conference and what will happen at that hearing?

- Case management & next steps
  - What does the judge usually expect at a status conference here? (e.g., scheduling, referrals, temporary relief)
  - What deadlines will likely be set (discovery, motion deadlines, mediation, expert disclosures, trial date)?
  - Is there a proposed case management order or scheduling order we should expect?

- Temporary orders & protections
  - Should we ask for temporary custody/parenting-time and child support orders before trial?
  - How can we request or modify temporary orders if needed?
  - If there are safety concerns, should we seek a protective order or supervised visitation?

- Custody specifics
  - What standard does the court uses to decide custody/parenting time here?
  - What evidence best supports my desired custody arrangement (parenting schedule, school records, witness statements)?
  - Will a custody evaluation or guardian ad litem be likely or required? What does that process look like?

- Child support and finances
  - How is child support calculated in this jurisdiction? What documents will you need (pay stubs, tax returns, daycare costs, health insurance)?
  - Do we need temporary support orders now? How are health insurance and childcare usually handled interimly?

- Discovery & evidence
  - What documents and communications should we collect now (texts, emails, social media, receipts, medical and school records)?
  - Should we preserve electronic evidence and how (screenshots, timeline, backups)?
  - Do we need to subpoena records (medical, school, employer)?

- Settlement & alternative dispute resolution
  - Is mediation required or recommended here? When does it typically happen?
  - What are likely settlement options or risks if we go to trial?

- Strategy, costs & timeline
  - What is your recommended strategy and why?
  - How long will this probably take and what are likely cost ranges for mediation, evaluations, and trial?
  - What are realistic outcomes and the main strengths/weaknesses of my case?

- Practical next steps for me
  - What do you need from me in the next 2 weeks to be ready for the status conference and follow-up deadlines?
  - Should I avoid any specific actions or communications with the other parent right now?

2) Documents to gather now (bring copies to your attorney and have electronic backups)
- Identification and existing court papers: petitions, temporary orders, prior agreements
- Financial documents: last 3–6 pay stubs, most recent 2 years tax returns (1040), W-2s, bank statements, retirement statements, mortgage/rent, monthly bills, proof of healthcare premiums
- Child-specific records: birth certificates, school records, IEPs, medical/mental-health records, prescriptions
- Parenting and time logs: detailed calendar of parenting time from past 6–12 months (dates/times missed, late pickups, etc.)
- Communication logs: texts, emails, social-media messages relevant to custody/support or safety issues (export/save them)
- Witness/contact list: names and brief descriptions of people who can support your version (teachers, coaches, babysitters)
- Any police reports, protective orders, or prior domestic-related filings
- Receipts for childcare, extraordinary child expenses, travel costs related to parenting time

3) What to expect at the status conference (typical)
- Court will confirm parties and counsel, and identify whether temporary relief is in place or needed
- Judge or magistrate will likely set a schedule: deadlines for discovery, disclosure of experts, mediation/settlement conference, and trial date
- Court may refer case to mediation or parenting education
- Court may order a custody evaluation or appoint a guardian ad litem if needed
- If either side asks, the court may set or modify temporary orders for custody, parenting time, support, or exclusive use of the home

4) Typical timeline and next steps in custody/support cases
- Initial pleadings filed and served
- Status conference / case management (sets deadlines)
- Temporary orders hearing (if needed) — for interim custody/support
- Discovery: exchange of financial info, witness lists, documents
- ADR: mediation/settlement conference (often before scheduling trial)
- Custody evaluations, GAL, or parenting assessments (if ordered)
- Pretrial motions and hearings (resolving disputes)
- Final trial or settlement
- Post-judgment motions/enforcement or modification later (child support and parenting can be modified based on changes)

5) Immediate practical actions you can take now
- Confirm and clarify which court is handling the case (Denver vs Hennepin); forward any court notices to your attorney
- Start compiling the documents above (financial, school, medical, communications)
- Keep a daily parenting log and calendar of events
- Preserve electronic evidence (export text threads, save emails as PDFs)
- Avoid heated or accusatory exchanges with the other parent — keep communications factual and documented
- Consider a professional evaluation (therapy notes, parenting courses) if it supports your case
- Ask your lawyer about intentionally limiting social media activity related to the case

6) Sample email you can send to Robert Kim (I can draft/send if you want)
Subject: Pre-status conference meeting/questions — [Your Name] (case re: [child names/ case # if available])

Hi Robert —

The judge set a status conference in about two months and I want to be prepared. Can we schedule a brief meeting or call before that date? I also have several questions I’d like to go over including confirming which court has jurisdiction (Denver vs Hennepin County), what will happen at the status conference, recommended temporary orders (custody/support), what documents you need from me, and an expected timeline/estimate of costs.

I can bring the following documents: paystubs, tax returns, bank statements, child school and medical records, and a parenting-time log. Please let me know if there’s anything else I should prepare or bring to our meeting.

Available times this week: [list 2–3 options]. If you prefer, you can just respond with next steps and any forms I should complete before the status conference.

Thanks,
[Your full name]
[Phone number]

Tell me whether you want that email written in a shorter or more formal tone and I’ll draft it exactly how you want.

7) Safety and urgent concerns
- If safety is a concern for you or the children, tell your attorney immediately and consider filing for immediate protective orders or supervised exchange arrangements.
- If there are allegations of abuse, preserve all relevant evidence and avoid direct contact — let your attorney handle communications.

---

If you confirm the court (Denver or Hennepin County) and whether you want me to draft/send the email to Robert Kim, I’ll prepare the exact email and a prioritized checklist you can print and bring to your meeting. I can also create a one-page summary of your case points and questions to hand to your lawyer at the status conference. Which would you like next?

