Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help with that — I’ll first clarify one detail, then give you a prioritized list of questions to ask Robert Kim, a document checklist to gather, a short plain-language timeline of typical next steps in family-court divorce/custody/support cases, and suggested immediate actions so you’ll be ready for the status conference.

Quick clarification
- You said proceedings are “at family court in Denver” and also “This is in Hennepin County, Minnesota.” Which is the actual court location for this case (Denver/Colorado or Hennepin County/Minnesota)? Jurisdiction matters a lot for procedure and support formulas, so please confirm.

Top questions to ask your attorney (prioritized)
1. Jurisdiction and venue
   - Are we properly in Hennepin County (or Denver)? Could the other parent try to change venue or assert another state’s jurisdiction?
2. Immediate safety / emergency orders
   - Should we seek any temporary restraining order, no-contact order, or emergency custody if there are safety concerns?
3. Temporary orders (custody & support)
   - What temporary custody/parenting time and child support arrangements should we request before trial?
   - How will temporary child support be calculated (guideline worksheet)? What about spousal support/maintenance?
4. Status conference purpose & preparation
   - What does the judge typically address at the status conference and what should we have ready or proposed for that hearing?
5. Discovery & disclosures
   - What financial and custody-related documents will the other side be required to produce and what should we request (pay stubs, tax returns, bank statements, debt, retirement, business records, school/medical records)?
   - Timeline for exchanging financial affidavits and disclosures.
6. Parenting plan and custody evaluation
   - Do you recommend mediation, a parenting coordinator, or a custody evaluation/guardian ad litem? How likely is the court to order one?
7. Strategy & goals
   - Based on the facts, what outcome do you recommend (primary custody, defined parenting time, supervised visits, relocation restrictions)? What’s realistic?
8. Evidence & witnesses
   - What evidence or witnesses will be most important (texts, call logs, third-party testimony, school reports, medical records, experts)?
9. Timeline to resolution and next steps
   - Typical schedule after the status conference (temporary orders hearing, discovery deadlines, mediation, pretrial, trial) and estimated timing to resolution.
10. Fees, costs, and settlement options
   - Estimated legal fees from here to trial, options for interim fee orders, and whether you recommend attempting settlement/mediation now.
11. Enforcement & modification
   - How are orders enforced and what is the process for future modifications if circumstances change?
12. Communication & next steps
   - Best way to communicate with you and preferred timeline for updates. What do you want from me before the status conference?

Documents to gather now (bring to your lawyer meeting)
- Child(ren)’s birth certificates and school enrollment info
- Marriage certificate (if available) / separation date
- Current court paperwork, petitions, summons, and any temporary orders already issued
- Recent pay stubs (last 3–6 months) for both parents if available
- Last 2–3 years tax returns (personal and business if applicable)
- Bank statements (last 6–12 months)
- Credit card statements, mortgage/loan documents, vehicle titles/leases
- Retirement/401(k)/pension statements, investment accounts
- Health insurance info (who covers children), medical bills for children
- Monthly household budget or expense list (housing, childcare, education, medical)
- Records of child-related expenses (childcare, tutoring, extracurriculars)
- Documentation of parenting time history (calendar, exchanges, missed visits)
- Relevant communications: text messages, emails, social-media messages about custody, parenting, finances
- Any police reports, protective-orders or prior court judgments
- School/medical/therapy records relevant to custody concerns
- Any other evidence of parental misconduct or factors relevant to best interests of the child (substance, neglect, domestic violence)

Typical next steps and a short timeline (what usually happens)
- Status conference (in two months): Court sets deadlines, may discuss temporary orders, discovery schedule, and ADR/mediation. Often a short hearing or scheduling conference.
- Temporary orders hearing (if needed, often before final resolution): Seeks interim custody, parenting time, child support, spousal support, and other needs.
- Discovery period: Exchange of financial affidavits, records, interrogatories, document requests, depositions if necessary.
- Alternative dispute resolution (mediation/settlement): Many courts require mediation for custody/parenting plan attempts before trial.
- Custody evaluations/experts (if ordered or requested): Psychological evaluations, parenting evaluations, or custody investigator.
- Pretrial motions and settlement talks: Motions on evidence, temporary relief, or disposition.
- Trial (if no settlement): Judge decides final custody and support orders.
- Post-judgment: Enforcement or modification can be requested later as circumstances change.

Practical actions to take now (before the status conference)
- Confirm the court location (and any deadlines you’ve been given).
- Gather the documents above and organize them in one folder or digital file.
- Prepare a short written timeline of key events and parenting schedule history (dates, missed visits, incidents).
- Draft a proposed parenting plan (your ideal schedule and a more conservative “acceptable” version).
- Track your expenses and create a simple monthly budget for child-related costs.
- Preserve communications (save texts, emails) and avoid inflammatory messages—communicate in writing only about logistics if possible.
- Ask your attorney whether to file any temporary-motion requests now (custody, support, exclusive use of home, etc.).
- Consider whether you want to propose mediation early.

Rights to discuss with your attorney (what you can ask for)
- Right to counsel and to present evidence/witnesses
- Right to request temporary orders for custody/support/exclusive use of the home
- Right to request financial disclosure and discovery
- Right to request a custody evaluation or guardian ad litem (if appropriate)
- Right to propose a parenting plan and be heard on child’s best interests
- Right to enforce and later request modification of orders
- Right to request interim attorney-fee awards in some circumstances

How I can help next
- I can draft a concise email or list of questions you can send to Robert Kim summarizing these points (I can draft it for you to review).
- I can help you organize the document checklist into an uploadable bundle or a checklist you can take to your meeting.
- I can draft a proposed parenting plan template based on the schedule you want.
- I can create a short timeline of key events you can hand to your attorney or the court.

Next step from you
- Confirm which court is handling the case (Denver or Hennepin County) and whether there are any existing temporary orders or deadlines already set.
- Tell me if you want me to draft the email to Robert Kim or a proposed parenting plan. If yes, say so and give any specific points you want included (tone: neutral/formal; list all questions or prioritize a few).

Would you like me to draft an email to Robert Kim now summarizing these questions and asking for a meeting/prep for the status conference? If yes, tell me any specific requests or tone and I’ll draft it.

