Below is a practical, organized plan you can use to get ready and to guide your conversation with Robert Kim. I’ll include suggested questions, a document checklist, what typically happens next, and suggested phrasing you can use in a meeting or email to your attorney.

Questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic)
- Jurisdiction & case basics
  - Which court is handling this case and are we asserting jurisdiction in Hennepin County (or Denver)? Will that affect deadlines or local rules?
  - What is the exact date/time of the status conference and what will be decided there?
  - Who is the judge or assigned magistrate (if known), and are there standing courtroom procedures I should know about?

- Immediate procedural steps & timeline
  - What are the next procedural steps between now and the status conference?
  - What deadlines (financial disclosures, motions, filings) should I expect in the next 30/60/90 days?
  - What should we have prepared for the status conference specifically (proposed orders, case management plan, parenting plan draft)?

- Custody / parenting-time issues
  - What are my legal rights regarding custody and parenting time under our jurisdiction’s statutes/guidelines?
  - How will the court evaluate “best interests” of the child here? What factors matter most?
  - Do you recommend a temporary parenting-time order now? If so, what terms should we seek?
  - If the other parent requests relocation or reduced parenting time, how do we respond/what evidence matters?

- Child support & financial support
  - How is child support calculated here? What income/documentation do we need to produce?
  - Are there temporary support options while the case is ongoing?
  - What about health insurance, daycare, extracurricular costs — how are those handled?

- Evidence, disclosure, and discovery
  - What documents should I gather now (see checklist below)?
  - Will we need to serve discovery (interrogatories, document requests) and when?
  - Should we seek subpoenas for records (school, medical, phone/location data)?
  - How do we preserve important evidence (text messages, social media, GPS, photos)?

- Settlement, mediation, and evaluations
  - Is mediation or a parenting evaluation (custody evaluation) likely or mandatory in this jurisdiction?
  - If mediation is recommended, when does it typically happen and who pays?
  - Under what circumstances do you recommend a custody evaluation or expert (psychologist, social worker, forensic accountant)?

- Strategy, risks, and costs
  - What are our realistic options and likely outcomes (settlement, temporary orders, trial)?
  - What are the main risks or weak points in our position?
  - What are estimated legal fees and costs for the next phases (discovery, mediation, trial)?
  - Are there ways to limit costs (narrow discovery, early mediation)?

- Safety and emergency issues
  - If there are safety concerns or domestic violence, what immediate steps should we take (restraining order, emergency hearing)?
  - How do we document and present safety concerns to the court?

- Practical next actions for you
  - What do you want me to do right now (prepare documents, draft proposed parenting plan, file a motion, schedule a meeting)?

Suggested documents to gather now
- Identification & case basics: court filings you’ve received, any petitions or temporary orders
- Parenting / custody: calendar of parenting time events (missed visits, incidents), school records, attendance, childcare/activities schedule
- Communication records: text message logs, emails, call logs, social media messages relevant to parenting or behavior (export or screenshot and save with dates)
- Financial records: last 3–6 months of pay stubs, last 2 years tax returns, bank statements, retirement account statements, mortgage/lease, credit card statements, recent bills (utilities, healthcare)
- Expenses for child: daycare, medical bills, tuition, extracurricular receipts
- Healthcare records: immunizations, significant medical reports
- Any evidence of concerning behavior: police reports, CPS reports, restraining orders, photos, witness names
- Parenting plan ideas: proposed schedule (weekdays/weekends/holidays), proposed pick-up/drop-off logistics, transportation plan
- Professional records: names/contact info of doctors, therapists, school counselors

What typically happens next (general timeline and expectations)
- Immediate to status conference (next 1–2 months)
  - Exchange of initial financial disclosures and parenting proposals (local rules vary)
  - Meet with your attorney to set goals and strategy; prepare documents for the status conference
  - Court may set a case management schedule (dates for mediation, discovery cutoff, trial window)

- Short-term after status conference (2–6 months)
  - Mediation or settlement discussions are often scheduled
  - Discovery (document exchange, written questions); possible temporary orders hearing if urgent
  - If high conflict, a court may order a custody evaluation or parenting classes

- Medium-term (6–12 months)
  - Depositions and expert reports (if needed)
  - Continued negotiation; some cases settle before trial
  - If no settlement, pretrial motions and trial scheduling

- Long-term (12+ months)
  - Trial (if necessary) and then final orders
  - Post-judgment modifications may occur later if circumstances change

Tips for your meeting with Robert Kim (practical)
- Bring a concise one-page timeline of key events (birthdates, separation date, major incidents, custody disputes)
- Bring a prioritized list of your goals (what you need vs. what you want)
- Ask for a clear, written next-step plan and a calendar of upcoming deadlines
- Request cost estimates for the next steps (mediation, custody eval, trial) so you can plan

Suggested phrasing you can use in the meeting or email
