Thanks — I can help you get organized and ready. First, quick clarification: you mention proceedings at family court in Denver and also that this is in Hennepin County, Minnesota. Which county/state is this matter actually filed in (Denver/Colorado or Hennepin County/Minnesota)? That affects jurisdiction, timing, and procedures. Tell me which one is correct and I’ll tailor the checklist. For now I’ll give a general, practical checklist and suggested questions you can bring to Mr. Kim that will be applicable in most U.S. family-court custody/support cases.

Suggested questions to ask your attorney
- Jurisdiction and status
  - Which court has jurisdiction here (Denver or Hennepin)? If there are filings in more than one state, what steps are needed to resolve that conflict?
  - What is the purpose of the upcoming status conference and what should I expect the judge to decide or schedule there?
- Custody/parenting time (legal and physical custody)
  - What custody standard does this court apply and how does that affect my rights (what factors are weighed)?
  - What are my options for a parenting plan or temporary parenting schedule now?
  - What evidence will be most important to support the parenting arrangement I want?
  - If relocation becomes an issue, how would it be handled?
- Child support and financial issues
  - How is child support calculated here (guidelines/formulas)? What documents will you need to prepare a child-support worksheet?
  - Should I request temporary support or temporary spousal support now (if applicable)?
  - What financial disclosures are required and what is the timetable for exchanging them?
- Temporary/emergency relief
  - Should we ask for any temporary orders (custody, parenting time, support, exclusive use of the home, restraining orders)?
  - What constitutes an emergency that would justify an immediate motion?
- Discovery and evidence
  - What discovery will we need (financial records, school records, medical, text messages, social media, witnesses)?
  - Will there be depositions, interrogatories, or subpoenas? What’s the timing?
  - Should we preserve any digital evidence now (texts, emails, social posts, GPS, call logs)?
- Evaluations and third parties
  - Will the court likely order a custody evaluation, guardian ad litem (GAL), or parenting investigator? How do those work and how long do they take?
  - If recommended, who pays for evaluations and what input will the evaluator have?
- Alternative dispute resolution
  - Is mediation or collaborative law required or encouraged before trial? Do you recommend mediation now?
  - What is your strategy for settlement vs preparing for trial?
- Case management and timeline
  - What is the likely timeline from the status conference to final resolution (approximate deadlines for discovery, motions, mediation, trial)?
  - What deadlines should I expect after the status conference?
- Costs, billing, and communication
  - What are your expected fees and likely costs for discovery and experts? How will billing work?
  - How do you prefer to communicate (email, phone)? What response times should I expect?
- Practical safety/parenting concerns
  - If I have safety concerns about the other parent, what steps should I take now?
  - How should I document breaches of parenting time or other incidents?
- Next best steps
  - What do you recommend my immediate next steps be in the next 2 months before the status conference?

What to prepare and bring to the status conference
- Short written summary of the case: one-page timeline (key dates, living arrangements, major incidents, prior orders).
- Proposed parenting plan: proposed schedule for weekdays, weekends, holidays, and school breaks.
- Financial documents:
  - Recent pay stubs, W-2s/1099s, tax returns (past 2 years), bank statements, mortgage/lease details, credit-card statements.
  - Monthly budget/expenses for household and child-related costs.
- Child-related documents: school records, IEPs, doctors’ records, daycare bills, extracurricular schedules.
- Evidence files: copies (or organized summaries) of texts, emails, photos, videos, voicemails, social posts, and any witness names and brief statements.
- List of questions you want the lawyer to raise at the status conference.
- Names and contact information for proposed witnesses or experts.
- Any prior court orders, pleadings, or temporary orders already in place.

What typically happens at a status conference (general)
- Court confirms parties and counsel and sets a schedule for the case: discovery deadlines, motion deadlines, mediation date, trial date window, etc.
- Court may discuss whether temporary orders are needed or continue existing temporary orders.
- Court may refer the matter to mediation or a custody evaluator, or order a parenting investigator/GAL in contested custody cases.
- The judge may set deadlines for disclosures (financial affidavits, exchange of documents) and require a proposed case management order.
- Often nothing final is decided at this hearing beyond scheduling and process — it’s primarily to set the roadmap.

Typical next steps after a status conference
- Exchange required financial disclosures and documents by the court-ordered dates.
- File or respond to any motions (temporary orders, discovery motions).
- Attend mediation or supervised negotiations (if ordered or agreed).
- Complete discovery (interrogatories, document requests, depositions) per schedule.
- If ordered, complete custody evaluations or parenting assessments.
- Attempt settlement; if settlement fails, prepare for trial (witness prep, exhibits, trial brief).
- Possible post-status-conference hearings on specific motions (emergency relief, sanctions, contempt).

Practical tips to protect your position
- Keep a private, contemporaneous calendar/log of parenting time exchanges, missed visits, and any concerns (dates, times, brief factual notes).
- Preserve digital evidence now — download and back up texts, voicemails, social posts, photos, and emails.
- Avoid posting about the case or the children on social media.
- Keep communications factual and limited with the other parent (document major interactions).
- If safety is an issue, get police reports and document incidents; inform your attorney immediately.
- Ask your attorney what to say (and what not to say) to the court and to the other parent.

How I can help right now
- I can draft a concise email or meeting agenda you can send to Robert Kim summarizing these questions and asking for time to discuss before the status conference.
- I can format a one-page timeline and checklist for you to bring to the meeting.
- I can create a printed question list you can bring to the status conference.

